What steps can reproduce the bug?
When bundling CSS with Bun's build API, multiple @supports blocks nested inside a single selector are incorrectly dropped. Only the first
@supports block is preserved in the output, breaking progressive enhancement patterns commonly used by modern CSS frameworks like Tailwind.
Minimal Reproduction:
  import { build } from "bun";

  await build({
    entrypoints: ["./input.css"],
    outdir: "./",
    target: "browser",
    minify: true,
  });

Input CSS:
  .test {
    border-top: 1px solid red;
    border-left: 1px solid red;
    border-bottom: 1px solid red;
    border-right: 1px solid red;
    box-shadow: none;

    @supports (color: blue) {
      border-top: 1px solid blue;
    }
    @supports (color: blue) {
      border-left: 1px solid blue;
    }
    @supports (color: blue) {
      border-bottom: 1px solid blue;
    }
    @supports (color: blue) {
      border-right: 1px solid blue;
    }
    @supports (color: blue) {
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px blue;
    }
  }

Expected Output:
All 5 @supports blocks should be preserved (can be extracted from the selector, but must all be present).
Actual Output:
  .test{box-shadow:none;border:1px solid red}@supports (color: blue){.test{border-top:1px solid #00f}}

Only 1 @supports block is present (border-top). The other 4 blocks (border-left, border-bottom, border-right, box-shadow) are completely
dropped.
Real-world Impact:
Tailwind CSS v4 uses this pattern for color-mix() fallbacks, generating multiple nested @supports blocks for progressive enhancement. Bun's
CSS bundler breaks these utilities by dropping all but the first enhancement.
Environment:

Bun v1.3.2
macOS 15.5 (Darwin 24.5.0)

Workaround:
// Don't bundle CSS with Bun
external: ["*.css"]
