const server = Bun.serve({
  port: 3456,
  fetch(req, server) {
    // Upgrade WITHOUT selecting a subprotocol
    server.upgrade(req);
  },
  websocket: {
    open(ws) {
      console.log("Server: opened");
    },
    message() {},
  },
});

const ws = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:3456", ["proto1", "proto2"]);
ws.onopen = () => {
  console.log("Client protocol:", ws.protocol); // Prints "proto1" instead of ""
  ws.close();
  server.stop();
};
What is the expected behavior?
When server.upgrade(req) is called without explicitly setting a Sec-WebSocket-Protocol response header, the server should NOT include that header in the upgrade response. Per RFC 6455, the client's ws.protocol should then be "" (empty string).
What do you see instead?
Bun's server automatically echoes the first client-offered protocol (proto1) in the Sec-WebSocket-Protocol response header, even though the server code never selected one.
Raw HTTP upgrade response captured:
HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
Sec-WebSocket-Accept: ...
Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: proto1   <-- Should NOT be present
This causes ws.protocol on the client to incorrectly report "proto1" when no protocol was actually selected by the application.
Additional information
This is related to #18243 but demonstrates a specific behavioral issue: Bun auto-selects the first offered protocol instead of requiring explicit selection. This breaks subprotocol negotiation semantics where:

Server should only echo a protocol it explicitly supports
Client logic checking ws.protocol === "" to detect "no protocol selected" fails silently
Applications enforcing strict protocol requirements pass validation incorrectly

Workaround: Explicitly set an empty or null protocol header (if supported), but this shouldn't be necessary per the WebSocket spec.

