Description
When using --define with bun test, identifier substitution works at module level and inside synchronous functions, but fails inside async function bodies with ReferenceError: <identifier> is not defined.
Minimal reproduction
// repro.test.ts
import { test, expect } from "bun:test"

// Works: module level
const moduleLevel = MACRO.VERSION

// Works: sync function
function syncFn() {
  return MACRO.VERSION
}

// Fails: async function body
async function asyncFn() {
  return MACRO.VERSION  // ReferenceError: MACRO is not defined
}

test("module level", () => {
  expect(moduleLevel).toBe("1.0.0")
})

test("sync function", () => {
  expect(syncFn()).toBe("1.0.0")
})

test("async function", async () => {
  expect(await asyncFn()).toBe("1.0.0")  // Fails here
})
Run with:
bun test --define 'MACRO={"VERSION":"1.0.0"}' repro.test.ts
Expected behavior
MACRO.VERSION should be substituted with "1.0.0" in all contexts, including inside async function bodies.
Actual behavior

Module level: ✅ works
Sync function body: ✅ works
Async function body: ❌ ReferenceError: MACRO is not defined

Workaround
Capture the defined value at module level before using it in async contexts:
const VERSION = MACRO.VERSION  // Capture at module level

async function asyncFn() {
  return VERSION  // Works
}
Environment
bun --version

Analysis
Looking at the bun source code:

src/ast/visitExpr.zig:141-183 handles define substitution by checking if a symbol is .unbound
There does not appear to be intentional differentiation between async and sync function handling
This suggests the issue is in symbol resolution timing or how async function bodies are transpiled

