Problem
When building database driver adapters (e.g., for Prisma), we need to know the PostgreSQL column types (OIDs) of query result columns. Currently, Bun.sql auto-parses values (JSONB → JS objects, dates → Date, etc.) but doesn't expose the underlying column type information.
This makes it impossible to distinguish between:

A jsonb column containing ["a", "b"] (should be treated as JSON)
A text[] column containing ["a", "b"] (should be treated as a text array)

Both produce identical JavaScript arrays, but need completely different handling when passed to consumers like Prisma's WASM engine.
Current workaround
We infer column types from JavaScript values (inferOidFromValue), which is fundamentally unreliable for ambiguous types like arrays.
What we need
A .columns property (or similar) on query results that exposes column metadata from PostgreSQL's RowDescription message. Bun already receives this data via the wire protocol — it just needs to be surfaced to JavaScript.
Desired API
const result = await sql`SELECT id, data, tags FROM posts WHERE id = ${1}`;

// Option A: .columns property on result
console.log(result.columns);
// [
//   { name: "id", typeOid: 23 },      // int4
//   { name: "data", typeOid: 3802 },   // jsonb
//   { name: "tags", typeOid: 1009 },   // text[]
// ]

// Option B: also works with .values() and unsafe()
const result2 = await sql.unsafe("SELECT * FROM posts").values();
console.log(result2.columns);
Minimal useful shape
Even just an array of OIDs would be sufficient:
result.columnOids // [23, 3802, 1009]
Context

SQLite already has Statement.columnTypes and Statement.declaredTypes — this is the PostgreSQL equivalent
The roadmap mentions "Column type transforms" — this feature is a prerequisite
postgres.js exposes this via result.statement.columns
pg exposes this via result.fields[].dataTypeID
This is critical for building reliable Prisma driver adapters with Bun.sql

Use case
We maintain @onreza/prisma-adapter-bun, a Prisma 7+ driver adapter for Bun.sql. Without column OIDs, JSONB columns containing arrays or primitive strings cause "JSON Parse error: Unexpected EOF" in Prisma's WASM engine because we can't reliably identify them as JSON.
