What steps can reproduce the bug?
const { execFileSync } = require("child_process");
try {
  execFileSync("nonexistent_binary_xyz_123");
} catch (err) {
  console.log("err.error === err:", err.error === err); // true
  JSON.stringify(err); // throws: cannot serialize cyclic structures
}
Also reproducible via spawnSync directly:
const { spawnSync } = require("child_process");
const ret = spawnSync("nonexistent_binary_xyz_123");
// Simulate what checkExecSyncError does:
const err = ret.error;
Object.assign(err, ret);
console.log("err.error === err:", err.error === err); // true
What is the expected behavior?
Error objects from failed execFileSync/execSync/spawnSync calls should not contain self-referencing cycles. JSON.stringify(err) should work.
What do you see instead?
err.error === err (the error points to itself), so JSON.stringify(err) throws TypeError: JSON.stringify cannot serialize cyclic structures.
Additional information
The bug is in checkExecSyncError in src/js/node/child_process.ts lines 1016-1018:
if (ret.error) {
    err = ret.error;          // err IS ret.error
    ObjectAssign(err, ret);   // copies ret.error onto err → err.error = err
}
ret has shape { error: <Error>, status, stdout, stderr, ... }. After line 1017, err and ret.error are the same object. ObjectAssign(err, ret) copies all of ret's properties onto err, including .error — so err.error points to err itself.
Node.js has the identical code and the same bug.
A minimal fix would be to delete the self-reference after the assign: delete err.error; after line 1018.

@alii core-js types are still under development. core-js provides polyfills for standard library features - from ancient to bleeding-edge. However, we don't know with what version of the standard library types in will be called, so we are forced to duplicate standard library types that are already present in TypeScript and Bun. And in case of loading core-js types with Bun types, we have errors because of conflicts:
Bun:
interface ArrayBuffer {
    resize(byteLength: number): ArrayBuffer;
}
core-js and TypeScript:
interface ArrayBuffer {
    resize(newByteLength?: number): void;
}
Here is an incorrect return type, take a look at the spec, https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-arraybuffer.prototype.resize
Bun:
interface PromiseConstructor {
  withResolvers<T>(): {
    promise: Promise<T>;
    resolve: (value?: T | PromiseLike<T>) => void;
    reject: (reason?: any) => void;
  };
core-js and TypeScript:
interface PromiseWithResolvers<T> {
    promise: Promise<T>;
    resolve: (value: T | PromiseLike<T>) => void;
    reject: (reason?: any) => void;
}

interface PromiseConstructor {
    withResolvers<T>(): PromiseWithResolvers<T>;
}
Unlike the previous case, it's not a direct error, it's a conflict that just gets in the way of using these types together.
