What is the problem this feature would solve?
When writing a Bun.plugin that needs to transform source code, the transformation often depends on whether the file is being loaded as an ES module (import) or as CommonJS (require). Currently, the onLoad callback's args object only provides path and namespace, there is no indication of how the file is being loaded.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Expose an importKind field (or similarly named, e.g., format, moduleType) on the args object passed to onLoad callbacks, indicating whether the current load was triggered by an import or a require() call.
Note: in the Bun docs the onLoad callback args object already has an ImportKind which would work well for this use case, the actual callback does not expose it.
Bun.plugin({
  name: 'my-plugin',
  setup(build) {
    build.onLoad({ filter: /\.js$/, namespace: 'file' }, (args) => {
      const format = args.importKind === 'require' ? 'commonjs' : 'module'
      const contents = transform(readFileSync(args.path, 'utf8'), format)
      return { contents, loader: 'js' }
    })
  },
})

What alternatives have you considered?
Alternatives involve plugin authors having to determine the module format ahead of time at plugin setup, using fragile heuristics such as inspecting file extensions (.mjs / .cjs), scanning path segments for conventions like esm or cjs, and walking up the directory tree to read package.json type fields.
These heuristics are inherently unreliable. The same .js file can be loaded as either ESM or CJS depending on the call site (import vs require()), not its location on disk. This is especially common with dual-format packages. Because the format cannot be determined at load time, plugin authors must pre sort files into two separate regex filters one for ESM and one for CJS and fall back to additional interception mechanisms as the onLoad callback currently breaks CJS code.

What is the problem this feature would solve?
When writing a Bun.plugin onLoad that transforms source code at load time, the plugin author most likely will need to read the file's contents before actually performing any transformation. Currently, the onLoad callback's args object only provides path and namespace, so every plugin must manually read the file contents (e.g using readFileSync).
This leads to repeated boilerplate code in which every source transforming plugin must import something like fs, call readFileSync, and handle the encoding. This is noise that obscures the plugin's actual purpose.
Bun already knows the resolved path, the expected format, and has an optimized internal file reading routines. Plugin authors are forced to go through Node compatible fs APIs instead of benefiting from Bun's internal fast paths.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Add an opt in option (e.g., { contents: true } on the filter object) that instructs Bun to pre read the file and pass its contents in the args object:
build.onLoad({ filter: /\.js$/, namespace: 'file', contents: true }, (args) => {
  const transformed = transform(args.contents, args.path)
  return { contents: transformed, loader: 'js' }
})

Making it opt in preserves the current behavior for plugins that don't need the contents or if needed for it to be read independently.
What alternatives have you considered?
The alternative is using readFileSync(args.path, 'utf8') in every onLoad callback, This is what virtually every source transforming plugin does today. It works, but it's boilerplate that obscures the plugin's actual purpose, introduces opportunities for encoding bugs, introduces startup time requires and bypasses any fast I/O paths Bun could use internally.

