Two files with identically-named namespace declarations, where one re-exports from the other:
a.ts
export namespace Foo {
  export const value = 42
}
b.ts
import { Foo as S } from "./a"
export namespace Foo {
  export const value = S.value
}
console.log(Foo.value)
bun run b.ts      # works: prints 42
bun build b.ts --outdir=dist --target=bun
bun run dist/b.js # broken: prints undefined
What is the expected behavior?
Both bun run b.ts and the bundled output should print 42.
What do you see instead?
The bundled output prints undefined. The generated code:
// a.ts
var Foo;
((Foo) => {
  Foo.value = 42;
})(Foo ||= {});

// b.ts
var Foo2;
((Foo) => {
  Foo.value = Foo.value;  // self-assignment, should reference outer Foo
})(Foo2 ||= {});
console.log(Foo2.value);
The IIFE parameter Foo shadows the outer Foo (from a.ts), so Foo.value = Foo.value reads its own uninitialized property instead of the value from a.ts. The bundler should generate something like Foo.value = Foo$1.value.
Same-named namespaces within a single file work fine. The bug only triggers when bundling across files. It also doesn't matter what's inside the namespace (values, functions, classes) or how you import (import { Foo as S }, import *). It only requires the two namespaces to share the same name.
Additional information
Using a different namespace name in the re-exporting file works around the issue. Plain ESM re-exports (no namespace) also work fine.

