When a Bun.serve handler returns a new Response(readableStream) (streaming response), and the client disconnects mid-stream, request.signal never fires its abort event. The ReadableStream.cancel() callback is also never called.
Bun.serve({
  port: 3000,
  fetch(request) {
    const stream = new ReadableStream({
      start(controller) {
        // Send a chunk every second
        const interval = setInterval(() => {
          controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode("data: ping\n\n"));
        }, 1000);

        // This never fires when client disconnects
        request.signal.addEventListener("abort", () => {
          console.log("abort fired"); // Never logged
          clearInterval(interval);
          controller.close();
        });
      },
      cancel() {
        console.log("cancel fired"); // Also never called
      },
    });

    return new Response(stream, {
      headers: { "Content-Type": "text/event-stream" },
    });
  },
});
To reproduce:
# Terminal 1: start server
bun run server.ts

# Terminal 2: connect and disconnect
curl -N http://localhost:3000 &
sleep 2
kill %1  # disconnect client
# Server never logs "abort fired" or "cancel fired"
What is the expected behavior?
When the client disconnects from a streaming response:

request.signal should fire its abort event
The ReadableStream.cancel() callback should be called

This is the standard mechanism for servers to detect client disconnection and clean up resources (cancel intervals, close database cursors, interrupt async work, etc.).
Node.js http.ServerResponse emits close in this scenario. Deno's Request.signal fires abort. Bun should match this behavior.
What do you see instead?
Neither request.signal abort nor ReadableStream.cancel() fires. The streaming response fiber/interval continues running indefinitely, causing:

Memory leaks (subscriptions, cursors, fibers accumulate)
CPU spin (producers keep writing to an abandoned stream)
Resource exhaustion on long-lived servers with many client disconnects

