What steps can reproduce the bug?
When using a TransformStream with an async transform function, flush() can fire while a previous transform() call is still awaiting an async operation. Per the WHATWG Streams spec, flush() should only be called after all pending transform() calls have completed.
Minimal reproduction:
let transformComplete = false;

const ts = new TransformStream({
  async transform(chunk, controller) {
    // Simulate async work
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100));
    transformComplete = true;
    controller.enqueue(chunk);
  },
  flush() {
    // Per spec, this should only fire after transform() resolves
    console.log('flush called, transformComplete:', transformComplete);
    // Expected: true
    // Actual in Bun: sometimes false
  }
});

const writer = ts.writable.getWriter();
const reader = ts.readable.getReader();

// Write and immediately close
writer.write('chunk1');
writer.close();

// Read all
while (true) {
  const { done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
}
What is the expected behavior?
flush() should only be called after all transform() Promises have resolved. The WHATWG Streams spec states:

The flush algorithm is called after all chunks written to the writable side have been transformed by successfully passing through transformer.transform()

So transformComplete should always be true when flush runs.
What do you actually see?
flush() fires while transform() is still awaiting. In our real-world case (Vercel AI SDK's runToolsTransformation), this causes tool execution results to be lost because flush closes the stream before transform has registered the tool call.
Trace from production code:
[transform] tool-call started, pending: 1
[flush] fired, pending: 0, outstanding: 0   ← transform hasn't finished!
[stream CLOSED]                              ← too early
[transform] tool-call finished, outstanding: 1  ← too late
[tool result] → nowhere to go

This is timing-dependent — it reproduces consistently when synchronous I/O (e.g., SQLite queries) occurs in the transform's async path, affecting microtask scheduling. It does not reproduce (or reproduces rarely) without the synchronous I/O.
