What steps can reproduce the bug?
When multiple imported modules are watched and any one of them is replaced via the standard atomic-write idiom (write temp file, rename(2) over the target — what vim, VSCode, prettier, etc. do), subsequent atomic writes to other imported modules stop propagating through --hot's live bindings, and the module graph begins to oscillate between old and new states.
I noticed this while developing a server application that was being modified by claude code. Claude kept getting confused as to why its changes weren't being applied, then I diagnosed with claude code into a reproducible issue (hopefully correct!)
Repro files:
// entry.js
import { a } from "./a.js";
import { b } from "./b.js";
import { c } from "./c.js";
setInterval(() => {
process.stdout.write([tick] ${a()} ${b()} ${c()}\n);
}, 50);
// a.js
export function a() { return "a-0"; }
// b.js
export function b() { return "b-0"; }
// c.js
export function c() { return "c-0"; }
Driver (each call = write to a temp file + rename):
atomicWrite(a.js, returning "a-1")    // works: ticks show "a-1 b-0 c-0"
atomicWrite(c.js, returning "c-1")    // works: ticks show "a-1 b-0 c-1"
atomicWrite(b.js, returning "b-1")    // BROKEN: ticks oscillate
udata-oscillation-repro.ts
What is the expected behavior?
After each atomic rewrite, the next [tick] line reflects all three latest function return values — [tick] a-1 b-1 c-1.
What do you see instead?
After the third atomic write, b() never returns "b-1"; instead the stdout stream cycles indefinitely through:
[tick] a-1 b-0 c-1   ← stable briefly
[tick] a-0 b-0 c-0   ← module graph reverts to original
[tick] a-1 b-0 c-0
[tick] a-1 b-0 c-1
[tick] a-0 b-0 c-0   ← reverts again
...
Additional information
Claude diagnosed as follows:

Root cause in src/Watcher.zig:flushEvictions. On macOS, evictions use MultiArrayList.swapRemove(item), which moves the last watchlist entry into the vacated slot. The moved entry's kqueue EV_ADD
registration, however, was made with udata = its_old_index at add time (addFileDescriptorToKQueueWithoutChecks) and is never rewritten. A subsequent kevent for that file returns the stale udata,
which onFileUpdate uses verbatim as the watchlist index — routing the event to the wrong slot, triggering a spurious eviction of whatever entry now lives at the stale index, which in turn
swapRemoves and compounds the corruption.
