Summary
When a process using the duckdb npm package exits — either via database.close() + process.exit(), or simply by reaching end-of-script — Bun segfaults in the Database C++ destructor. The destructor calls Napi::MemoryManagement::AdjustExternalMemory(env, -bytes_allocated) using a napi_env stored at construction time. Bun tears down the napi_env before native addon destructors run, so the env pointer is stale at that point — a use-after-free.
Node.js does not exhibit this crash because it tears down NAPI objects in a different order: addon destructors fire while napi_env is still valid.

Root Cause
In duckdb/src/database.cpp:
// stored at construction time
Database::Database(...) : env(info.Env()) { ... }

// called during GC / process shutdown — env may already be invalid
Database::~Database() {
    Napi::MemoryManagement::AdjustExternalMemory(env, -bytes_allocated);
}
env is a raw napi_env pointer saved in the constructor. When Bun shuts down, it invalidates the napi_env before running C++ destructors for ObjectWrap-derived objects. The destructor then dereferences a freed env, segfaulting at a low address (typically 0x27 — an offset into the already-freed env struct).
This is a distinct bug from the GC/External UAF fixed in @duckdb/node-api@1.5.1-r.2. That fix addressed a missing Napi::Reference keeping a JS object alive during an async worker. This bug is about Bun's NAPI shutdown ordering — the napi_env itself is gone before ~Database() runs.

