The bug is that any pipe fd allocated outside of Bun.spawn (e.g., via bun:ffi calling libc pipe(2), or via node:net socket pair) cannot be drained by Bun's stream consumers. The advertiser side writes bytes successfully (kernel wchar increments) but the reader side never observes them.
Minimal repro (adapted from a multi-process integration test that hit this):
// repro.ts
import { dlopen, FFIType } from 'bun:ffi';

// 1. Allocate a pipe via libc pipe(2).
const libc = dlopen('libc.so.6', {
  pipe: { args: [FFIType.ptr], returns: FFIType.i32 },
});
const fds = new Int32Array(2);
const rc = libc.symbols.pipe(fds);
if (rc !== 0) throw new Error('pipe(2) failed');
const [readFd, writeFd] = [fds[0]!, fds[1]!];

// 2. Spawn a writer subprocess that inherits writeFd as its fd 3 and writes a line.
const writer = Bun.spawn(
  ['bun', '-e', `Bun.file(3).writer().write("hello\\n"); await Bun.file(3).writer().flush();`],
  {
    stdio: ['pipe', 'inherit', 'inherit', writeFd],
  },
);
await writer.exited;

// 3. Try to read the bytes from readFd via Bun.file(<fd>).stream().
const stream = Bun.file(readFd).stream();
const reader = stream.getReader();

// EXPECTED: { value: <Uint8Array of "hello\n">, done: false }
// ACTUAL:   never resolves — promise hangs indefinitely.
const result = await Promise.race([
  reader.read(),
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve('TIMEOUT_5s'), 5000)),
]);
console.log('result:', result);
Run with: bun run repro.ts. Observed output: result: TIMEOUT_5s. Expected: the hello\n chunk surfaces immediately because the writer subprocess has already written + flushed + exited.
Verification that the bytes really did reach the kernel pipe (between steps 2 and 3): inspect /proc/<writer_pid>/io — wchar shows the bytes were written. After step 3 hangs, inspect the parent's /proc/<self>/io — rchar does not increment, confirming Bun's stream consumer never issued read(2) on the pipe fd.
In our codebase the same pattern was hit in three different JS-layer wrappers around the read-end were tried; all three exhibit the bug:

Bun.file(readFd).stream() → flaky: ~25-50% of runs the bytes eventually surface (after seconds), the rest hang past the test timeout.
Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(null, { fd: readFd, autoClose: false })) → deterministically hangs the reader; bytes never surface.
