When mock.module(spec, factory) is called, Bun rebinds the exports of the already-captured module namespace object in-place. A captured await import(spec) reference taken before the mock therefore sees its function exports replaced by the stub's exports.
This silently breaks the common partial-stub pattern where the stub captures the real module and delegates back to it for the methods it doesn't override:
// real.ts
export function greet(): string {
  return "real";
}
// repro.test.ts
import { test, expect, mock } from "bun:test";

const real = await import("./real");

mock.module("./real", () => ({
  ...real,
  // Intent: forward to the real implementation for any call we don't
  // special-case. Reality: `real.greet` has been rebound to THIS stub by
  // mock.module, so the call recurses into itself forever.
  greet: () => `wrapped: ${real.greet()}`,
}));

const stubbed = await import("./real");

test("partial-stub delegation", () => {
  // Expected: "wrapped: real"
  // Actual:   test never returns — infinite recursion, event loop hangs.
  expect(stubbed.greet()).toBe("wrapped: real");
});
Run bun test repro.test.ts and the suite hangs indefinitely (kill with SIGTERM).
What is the expected behavior?
real.greet() inside the stub factory should call the original implementation, since real was captured before mock.module was registered. The test should pass with "wrapped: real". This matches Node's ESM mock loader behavior, vitest's vi.mock, and Jest's jest.mock — all of which keep the captured namespace pointed at the original module.
What do you see instead?
Infinite recursion; the test never resolves. No stack-overflow error is thrown — the recursion appears to be deferred through Bun's internal module-resolution path, so the event loop hangs cleanly without producing a diagnostic.
