I'm building an unbundled ESM dev server that transpiles individual .tsx files on the fly for HMR (same pattern Vite uses). For React HMR to work, the transpiled output needs $RefreshReg$ / $RefreshSig$ calls injected so react-refresh/runtime can register components and track hook signatures.
Bun.build() injects these correctly. new Bun.Transpiler() does not — and there appears to be no way to opt in.
const src = `
export function App() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);
  return <h1>{n}</h1>;
}
`;

// 1. Bun.build — Fast Refresh transform is applied
await Bun.write("/tmp/app.tsx", src);
const built = await Bun.build({
  entrypoints: ["/tmp/app.tsx"],
  target: "browser",
});
console.log(await built.outputs[0].text());
// → output contains $RefreshReg$(App, "App") and $RefreshSig$()

// 2. Bun.Transpiler — same input, no Fast Refresh calls injected
const t = new Bun.Transpiler({ loader: "tsx" });
console.log(t.transformSync(src));
// → plain JSX-stripped output, no $RefreshReg$ / $RefreshSig$ anywhere
What is the expected behavior?
Per-file transpilation via Bun.Transpiler should be able to produce React Fast Refresh-compatible output, the same way Bun.build() does. This is the natural API for dev-server use cases that re-transpile a single changed file (~0.1ms) instead of running a full bundle on every save.
What do you see instead?
The Fast Refresh transform is never applied through Bun.Transpiler, regardless of loader (tsx, jsx) or any option I've tried. The output is identical with or without React component definitions present — no $RefreshReg$, no $RefreshSig$. The TypeScript types for TranspilerOptions also don't surface anything related to Fast Refresh, so there's no obvious knob to flip.
This forces dev servers to fall back to running Bun.build() per file change, which is significantly slower than a direct transpile and pulls in bundler machinery that isn't needed for serving individual ESM modules.
