Summary
On Windows, vmDeallocatePhysicalPages() in Source/bmalloc/bmalloc/VMAllocate.h uses VirtualAlloc(MEM_RESET) rather than VirtualFree(MEM_DECOMMIT). This means that when bmalloc/JSC marks heap pages as idle, the physical frames and commit charge are not returned to the OS. On Linux the equivalent call uses madvise(MADV_DONTNEED), which does release physical frames. The result is a significant and permanent gap in observable memory usage between Linux and Windows for long-running Bun processes.
A second related gap: OSAllocator::hintMemoryNotNeededSoon() is an empty stub on Windows, so scavenger hints are silently dropped.
Evidence
Analysed via a memory dump (procdump -ma) of a long-running bun process (Claude Code --agent session, ~12 hours uptime, 864 MB private committed). VirtualQueryEx showed 329 MB of committed private pages containing only zero bytes — pages that bmalloc had called vmDeallocatePhysicalPages() on, which zeroed them via MEM_RESET but did not release the commit charge.
On Linux, madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) on the same pages would have returned the physical frames to the OS and dropped RSS by ~329 MB. On Windows those pages stayed committed and invisible to the GC.
Root cause in source
Source/bmalloc/bmalloc/VMAllocate.h
Linux path:
inline void vmDeallocatePhysicalPages(void* p, size_t vmSize)
{
    vmValidatePhysical(p, vmSize);
    SYSCALL(madvise(p, vmSize, MADV_DONTNEED));   // releases physical frames + reduces RSS
#if BOS(LINUX)
    SYSCALL(madvise(p, vmSize, MADV_DONTDUMP));
#endif
}
Windows path:
inline void vmDeallocatePhysicalPages(void* p, size_t vmSize)
{
    vmValidatePhysical(p, vmSize);
    bool writable = true;
    bool executable = true;
    VirtualAlloc(p, vmSize, MEM_RESET, protection(writable, executable));
    // MEM_RESET zeros the page contents but does NOT release commit charge.
    // Physical frames and private bytes are NOT returned to the OS.
}
MEM_RESET is not semantically equivalent to MADV_DONTNEED. From the Windows docs:

MEM_RESET — Indicates that data in the memory range is no longer of interest … the pages are not zeroed … the process is not notified. However, the virtual memory reservation is maintained.

The commit charge remains. RSS does not drop.
Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/OSAllocator.cpp (Windows)
void OSAllocator::hintMemoryNotNeededSoon(void*, size_t) { }
// Empty stub — scavenger hints are silently ignored on Windows.
On POSIX this calls madvise(MADV_DONTNEED). On Windows it does nothing.
