Going through `http.get` actually masks the issue with a *second* Bun bug (see "Note 2" below). When you skip the client and read the wire directly:

```js
const http = require("node:http");
const net = require("node:net");
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { "X-Multi": ["a", "b", "c"] });
  res.end("hi");
});
server.listen(0, () => {
  const port = server.address().port;
  const sock = net.connect(port, "127.0.0.1", () => {
    sock.write("GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: x\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n");
  });
  let buf = "";
  sock.on("data", d => (buf += d));
  sock.on("end", () => {
    const count = (buf.match(/^X-Multi:/gim) || []).length;
    console.log("wire X-Multi count:", count);
    server.close();
  });
});
```

Bun prints `wire X-Multi count: 1`, Node prints `3`. Confirmed: this is a server-side wire-format issue, not a client parsing artifact.

### Root cause

In `node:http` (`src/js/`) and `Bun.serve` (`src/jsc/bindings/NodeHTTP.cpp`), response headers are stored in WebKit's `WebCore::FetchHeaders` (the WHATWG `Headers` object).

`appendToHeaderMap` in `src/jsc/bindings/webcore/FetchHeaders.cpp:92` joins values with `", "` for any header except `Set-Cookie`:

```cpp
combinedTemp = makeString(existing, ", "_s, normalizedValue);
```

So when Bun calls `impl->set(name, val[0])` and then `impl->append(name, val[i])` for each subsequent array element (in `jsHTTPSetHeader`), the u

