```
req.rawHeaders: ["Host","x","X-Mixed-CASE","alpha","x-LOWER-mixed","beta","Connection","close"]
req.headers: {"host":"x","x-mixed-case":"alpha","x-lower-mixed":"beta","connection":"close"}
req.headersDistinct: {"host":["x"],"x-mixed-case":["alpha"],"x-lower-mixed":["beta"],"connection":["close"]}
```

### Actual (Bun 1.4.0)

```
req.rawHeaders: ["host","x","x-mixed-case","alpha","x-lower-mixed","beta","connection","close"]
req.headers: {"host":"x","x-mixed-case":"alpha","x-lower-mixed":"beta","connection":"close"}
req.headersDistinct: undefined
```

### Two findings

1. **`req.rawHeaders` lowercases names**, contrary to [Node's docs](https://nodejs.org/api/http.html#messagerawheaders) ("Header names are not lowercased, and duplicates are not merged"). Confirmed not a client artifact — the wire is read with raw `net.connect`. Same root cause as #20433.

2. **`req.headersDistinct` is `undefined`**. Per [Node's docs](https://nodejs.org/api/http.html#messageheadersdistinct), this should be a per-name array dictionary parallel to `req.headers`. Frameworks that walk this for multi-value headers (`Set-Cookie` parsing on the request side, etc.) get `undefined.something is not a function`.

