Repro

The MySQL adapter decides whether to omit the SET keyword for an update helper by checking the query suffix case-sensitively:

```ts
// src/js/internal/sql/mysql.ts
isUpsertUpdate(query: string): boolean {
  return query.trimEnd().endsWith("ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE");
}
```

SQL keywords are case-insensitive, so a lowercase spelling slips past the check and the normalizer inserts an extra SET, producing invalid SQL:

```ts
await sql`INSERT INTO t ${sql({ id: 1 })} on duplicate key update ${sql({ age: 31 })}`;
// generates: ... on duplicate key update SET `age` = ?
```

The surrounding command detection (detectCommand) already lowercases its input, so this check is the odd one out. The behavior predates #31994 (the check was moved verbatim from the old inline normalizeQuery); fixing it is a behavior change that was kept out of that dedup on purpose.

Fix: compare case-insensitively, for example `query.trimEnd().toUpperCase().endsWith("ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE")`, plus a regression test covering mixed-case spellings.

In the postgres and mysql pool (now src/js/internal/sql/shared.ts, previously duplicated in postgres.ts and mysql.ts), the user-provided onconnect and onclose callbacks run before the pool updates its own bookkeeping:

```ts
protected handleConnected(err: any) {
  ...
  if (connectionInfo?.onconnect) {
    connectionInfo.onconnect(err); // user code; may throw
  }
  this.storedError = err;          // never reached on throw
  this.state = err ? closed : connected;
  ...
  this.adapter.release(this, true);
}
```

If the callback throws, handleConnected/handleClose abort early: state stays pending, storedError is not recorded, pending queries are never notified, and release() never runs, so callers waiting on the pool can hang. The same applies to onclose in handleClose (queries are not failed, onFinish is not called).

This also means the hole-guard in hasConnectionsAvailable() is the only scan protected against re-entrancy during startup; isConnected(), flush(), and the private close() scan unguarded, which only matters through this same re-entrancy path.

The ordering is long-standing (it predates #31994, which moved the two identical copies into a shared base class without changing behavior). Hardening it means either updating internal state before invoking the hooks or wrapping the hook calls so an exception cannot abort bookkeeping, plus a test with a throwing onconnect/onclose.

In the postgres and mysql pool close() (now src/js/internal/sql/shared.ts, previously duplicated in postgres.ts and mysql.ts), the timeout option is gated on truthiness:

```ts
let timeout = options?.timeout;
if (timeout) {           // 0 is falsy, so timeout: 0 takes the else branch
  ...
  if (timeout === 0 || !this.hasPendingQueries()) {
    // close immediately   <- unreachable for 0
  }
  ...
} else {
  // graceful drain
}
```

The inner `timeout === 0` branch shows the intent: a zero timeout should force an immediate close. Because `if (timeout)` treats 0 as absent, `sql.close({ timeout: 0 })` with in-flight queries instead waits for a graceful drain, identical to calling close() with no options.

Long-standing behavior (predates #31994, which moved the two identical copies into a shared base class without changing behavior). Fix: gate on presence (`options?.timeout !== undefined`) so 0 flows through validation and the immediate-close branch, plus a test that close({ timeout: 0 }) settles without waiting for an in-flight query.

