### What

Every async `node:fs` operation that receives a Buffer/TypedArray/ArrayBuffer path argument leaks one GC root for that argument. The value stays protected for the lifetime of the process, so the path buffer (or its shared Buffer pool backing) is never collected.

### Mechanism

For async ops, `PathLike::from_js_with_allocator` (src/runtime/node/types.rs) roots the argument with `arguments.protect_eat()`. At schedule time, `to_thread_safe()` (src/jsc/node_path.rs, `Buffer` arm) protects the same value a second time. When the task completes, `ThreadSafe<A>`'s drop runs `PathLike::unprotect()`, which unprotects once.

On the success path, `run_async` (src/runtime/node/node_fs_binding.rs) intentionally never drops the `ManuallyDrop<ArgumentsSlice>`, so the `protect_eat` root is never released there either. Net effect: two protects, one unprotect, +1 permanent root per call. `gcProtect` is refcounted, so repeated calls with the same buffer accumulate.

The behavior matches the Zig implementation (shipped 1.3.x), where `protectEat` plus `toThreadSafe`'s `protect()` are also released by a single `deinitAndUnprotect`, so this predates the Rust port.

### Repro

```js
import fs from "node:fs";
import { heapStats } from "bun:jsc";

async function run(n, tag) {
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const p = Buffer.from("/tmp/nonexistent-" + tag + "-" + i);
    await fs.promises.access(p).catch(() => {});
  }
}

await run(2000, "warm");
Bun.gc(true);
const a = heapStats().objectTypeCounts.Uint8Array ?? 0;
await run(5000, "main");
Bun.gc(true);
const b = heapStats().objectTypeCounts.Uint8Array ?? 0;
console.log("before:", a, "after:", b, "delta:", b - a);
```

Output on 1.4.0-canary.1 (73ab3ddc7):

```
before: 2001 after: 7001 delta: 5000
```

One leaked `Uint8Array` per call, surviving `Bun.gc(true)`.

### Notes on a fix

Dropping either of the two protects balances the books, but which one is load-bearing differs by mode: the `protect_eat` root is what the sync path releases via `ArgumentsSlice`'s `Drop`, while the async path relies on `to_thread_safe`/`ThreadSafe::drop`. A fix needs to audit both modes plus the other argument types that call `to_thread_safe` on buffers without going through `protect_eat` (e.g. `StringOrBuffer`, which protects explicitly at parse time).

Found while working on #32189, which covers the separate resizable-ArrayBuffer crash; after that PR, snapshotted (resizable-backed) paths no longer take the `protect_eat` root, but fixed-len

