    headers: { *[Symbol.iterator]() { throw new Error("HBOOM") } },
  }).catch(e => console.log("REJECTED", e.message));
} catch (e) { console.log("SYNC THROW:", e.message); }
```

## What is the expected behavior?
`REJECTED HBOOM`. Per WHATWG `fetch()` step 3: *"Let requestObject be the result of invoking the initial value of Request as constructor with input and init. If this throws an exception, reject p with it and return p."* Argument-conversion exceptions reject the returned promise; `fetch()` never throws synchronously.

## What do you see instead?
`SYNC THROW: HBOOM` — the `.catch` never attaches, so a handled rejection becomes an uncaught synchronous exception. Any handler in Bun's own native option extraction (`src/runtime/webcore/fetch.rs`) that runs user JS via `?`-propagation has the same shape: `headers` (an iterable/getter), and — after #29103's fix — `proxy.url` (a `toString`/`Symbol.toPrimitive`). Every *validation* failure in that same block already correctly builds a rejected promise; only genuine user-JS exceptions escape synchronously.

## Additional information
Filed while landing the #29103 fix. That PR does not change this: it makes `proxy.url` consistent with the `headers` sibling's existing behavior. The real fix is at the entrypoint boundary — convert any pending exception raised during option extraction into a rejection of the returned promise, once, for the whole class.

## What happens

On macOS arm64, `bun:ffi` lays out **stack-passed arguments** using standard-AAPCS64 8-byte slots — one slot per argument. But Apple's arm64 ABI [deviates from standard AAPCS64 here](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/writing-arm64-code-for-apple-platforms#Pass-arguments-to-functions-correctly): stack arguments are packed at their **natural size and alignment**, so two consecutive C `int`s share a single 8-byte slot.

Any signature whose 9th-or-later integer-class arguments put a sub-8-byte type before further arguments hands the callee shifted garbage. For:

```c
int f(void* res, uint64_t a, uint64_t b,
      int s0, int s1, int p0, int p1, int d0,   // x0..x7 (registers)
      int d1, int g, uint64_t stream);          // stack
```

the callee (clang, Apple ABI) expects `d1 @ sp+0` (4 B), `g @ sp+4` (4 B), `stream @ sp+8`. Bun writes `d1 @ sp+0`, `g @ sp+8`, `stream @ sp+16` — so the callee reads `g = 0` (the empty upper half of d1's slot) and `stream = <g's value>`.

We hit this binding `mlx_conv2d` from [mlx-c](https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx-c) (exactly this signature shape): the shifted read turns the `mlx_stream` handle into a small integer and mlx segfaults inside the call at ~`0x1`. It looks exactly like a bad binding, not an ABI bug, which makes it nasty to diagnose. Crash report from the real call: https://bun.report/1.3.14/Ma10d9b296AggggC____my5ilDmy5ilDurh5kDuov5tCmkk51Cuw8+I+6431Cmkl51CmpyoyC+0xoyCu6jlzCm6voyC+8l11C+/y34C+qy34C+5sy6CA2AC

