### Summary

Bun's inspector protocol delivers `TestReporter.found`/`start`/`end` events
to a connected frontend via a background "debugger thread" that the main
(inspected) thread hands messages off to. If `bun test`'s main thread calls
`exit()` before that handoff has actually reached the frontend socket, the
undelivered events are gone — not delayed, not retryable, gone. This shows
up in two forms, both instances of the same underlying defect:

1. **Trailing-event loss** (mild, single-core-reproducible): a normally
   reading consumer loses a handful of events right at the end of a run —
   the last synchronous test(s)' `start`/`end` events queue and the process
   falls straight through to `exit()` in the same tick, racing a
   not-yet-executed handoff task.
2. **Bulk loss under backpressure** (severe, deterministic, dramatic): a
   consumer that can't keep up with the event stream — e.g. one that's
   paused or simply slower than the producer — loses the vast majority of
   events, because a WebSocket write reporting "still buffered, will
   deliver later" is mishandled as "delivered," so nothing is retried, and
   whatever's still in flight when the process exits is discarded outright.

### Reproduction (the dramatic case)

Config: `bun test` with `--inspect-wait`, 300 files × 60 tests each (18,000
trivial synchronous tests), each test logging a ~500-byte console payload.
The inspector client connects, completes the `Inspector.enable` /
`TestReporter.enable` / `Inspector.initialized` handshake, then **stops
reading the socket** (`socket.pause()`) and does not resume until after the
child process has already exited. No Docker, no CPU pinning — reproduces on
a plain, otherwise-idle host.

