### What steps can reproduce the bug?

`Database.exec()` with a multi-statement string: when one statement fails with certain SQLite errors (the reliable reproducer is `ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMN … NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))` on a populated table → `Cannot add a column with non-constant default`), the error is **silently swallowed and execution continues with the following statements** — unless the failing statement is the absolute final token of the string. Any trailing content flips it to silent: another statement, a line comment, or **even a single trailing newline**.

Other error classes (e.g. `no such table`) throw from every position, so the swallow is error-class-specific, which makes it easy to miss in tests.

```ts
import { Database } from "bun:sqlite";

function attempt(label: string, sql: string) {
  const db = new Database(":memory:");
  db.exec("CREATE TABLE t (x INTEGER)");
  let threw: string | null = null;
  try { db.exec(sql); } catch (e: any) { threw = e.message; }
  const rows = db.query("SELECT x FROM t ORDER BY x").all().map((r: any) => r.x);
  console.log(`${label}\n  threw: ${threw ?? "NO (silent)"}\n  rows in t: [${rows}]\n`);
  db.close();
}

const ALTER = "ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN c TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))";

attempt("E) failing ALTER in the MIDDLE",
  `INSERT INTO t VALUES (1); ${ALTER}; INSERT INTO t VALUES (2);`);

attempt("G) failing ALTER LAST, no trailing content",
  `INSERT INTO t VALUES (1); ${ALTER}`);

attempt("H) failing ALTER LAST, trailing newline",
  `INSERT INTO t VALUES (1); ${ALTER};\n`);

attempt("I) failing ALTER LAST, trailing line comment",
  `INSERT INTO t VALUES (1); ${ALTER}; -- end\n`);

attempt("A) contrast: 'no such table' in the MIDDLE throws correctly",
  "INSERT INTO t VALUES (1); SELECT * FROM no_such_table; INSERT INTO t VALUES (2);");
```

