I traced a gap in how Bun protects off-thread reads of `ArrayBuffer`/typed-array bytes. The shared native helper `JSC__JSValue__borrowBytesForOffThread` (`src/jsc/bindings/bindings.cpp`) hands a worker thread a raw pointer into a JS-supplied buffer and calls JSC's `ArrayBuffer::pin()` to keep the storage from moving. The comment right above it names its callers explicitly:

```
// at the cost of a transfer that silently no-ops for as long as a borrowing
// op (zlib, fs, crypto, shell, Bun.Image, SQL blob binds, ...) happens to
// be in flight over that buffer.
```

`pin()` only stops `.transfer()`/detach and GC-driven moves. It does nothing for a non-shared `WebAssembly.Memory`: `memory.grow()` reallocates the memory's backing pages, and those pages belong to the `Memory` object, not to the `ArrayBuffer` sitting on top of it. I found the currently open PR #37944 ("Copy instead of pinning ArrayBuffer storage a pin cannot hold in place") fixing exactly this hazard, and its own description spells it out:

> the buffer of a non-shared `WebAssembly.Memory` (its pages belong to the memory, which `memory.grow()` and the memory's own lifetime manage) ... are not held in place by a pin

That PR covers async `node:fs` read/write, native zlib/brotli/zstd, `crypto.scrypt`/`pbkdf2`, `CompressionStream`, `Bun.markdown`, the HTTP parser and shell redirects. I grepped the repo for every caller of `borrowBytesForOffThread` and there are exactly three: `bindings.cpp` itself, `src/runtime/image/Image.rs`, and `src/sql_jsc/mysql/MySQLValue.rs`. Neither of the last two is mentioned anywhere in #37944, and neither has a WASM-memory guard.

**`Bun.Image`** rejects resizable and shared `ArrayBuffer`s at construction (`Image.rs`, around line 406):

```rust
if ab.resizable || ab.shared {
    return Err(global.throw_invalid_arguments(format_args!(
