### What steps can reproduce the bug?

```ts
import { Database } from "bun:sqlite";

const db = new Database(":memory:");
console.log(db.query("SELECT sqlite_version()").get());

// loadExtension fails on macOS because Apple's system SQLite
// is compiled with SQLITE_OMIT_LOAD_EXTENSION
try {
  db.loadExtension("some_extension");
} catch (e) {
  console.log("loadExtension failed:", e.message);
}
```

On Linux/Windows, `bun:sqlite` statically links Bun's own bundled SQLite amalgamation (kept current, currently ~3.53.0 per recent release notes). On **macOS**, `bun:sqlite` instead dlopens Apple's system-provided `/usr/lib/libsqlite3.dylib` at runtime instead of statically linking Bun's bundled build.

### What is the expected behavior?

`sqlite_version()` should return Bun's bundled/current SQLite version (e.g. `3.53.0`) on macOS just like it does on Linux/Windows, and `Database.loadExtension()` should work out of the box without any extra setup.

Statically linking Bun's own bundled SQLite on macOS (instead of dlopen-ing Apple's system library) would fix both:
- Version consistency: `sqlite_version()` matches what release notes claim, on every platform.
- `loadExtension()` works out of the box, since Apple's system build has `SQLITE_OMIT_LOAD_EXTENSION` compiled in and Bun's own bundled build does not.

This also fixes version-dependent bugs that only reproduce on macOS because of the old system SQLite, e.g. the FTS5 `SQLITE_CORRUPT_VTAB` issue in #31247.

### What do you see instead?

- `sqlite_version()` returns an old, OS-frozen version (3.37.0–3.43.2 depending on macOS release) instead of Bun's bundled version.
- `db.loadExtension(...)` fails/throws (or previously segfaulted, see #5756) because Apple's system SQLite omits extension-loading support.
- The only documented workaround (https://bun.com/docs/runtime/sqlite#loadextension) requires every end user to `brew install sqlite` and call `Database.setCustomSQLite(path)` before creating any `Database` — i.e. anyone shipping a Bun app that uses SQLite extensions requires macOS users to separately install a new SQLite binary. This runs against Bun's "batteries included" philosophy: the same script behaves differently on a fresh macOS machine vs. Linux/Windows purely because of which libsqlite3 gets loaded.

