Node and Deno stay flat because they stop accepting writes.

`CompressionStream("gzip")`, `CompressionStream("deflate")` and `DecompressionStream` behave the same way.

### What is the expected behavior?

`CompressionStream` is defined as a `TransformStream` created with the default writable queuing strategy, i.e. a high water mark of 1 chunk. `writer.ready` must therefore stop resolving once a chunk is queued and nothing drains the readable side. That is what Node and Deno do, and what Bun's own `TransformStream` does.

### Why it matters

This is the difference between a constant memory cost and a memory cost proportional to the input. The natural way to compress a large file is to pipe it, and every producer that honors `writer.ready` is told to keep going. In zip.js the same behavior made a 256 MB entry peak at ~390 MB instead of ~100 MB. We now drive the codec writable by hand and await each `write()`, which paces the source correctly, but that workaround only exists b

