const other = new SQL({ url, max: 1 });

await sql`select set_config('demo.marker', 'session-one', false)`;
await sql`create temp table demo_marker (note text)`;

const [before] = await sql`
  select pg_backend_pid() as pid, current_setting('demo.marker', true) as marker
`;

await other`select pg_terminate_backend(${before.pid})`;
await Bun.sleep(400);

const [after] = await sql`
  select pg_backend_pid() as pid, current_setting('demo.marker', true) as marker
`;
console.log(before, after);
await sql`select count(*) from demo_marker`;
```
Output:
```
before: { pid: 12374, marker: "session-one" }
after:  { pid: 12376, marker: null }
backend silently replaced: true
temp table gone: relation "demo_marker" does not exist
```

The query succeeds, so nothing signals that session settings, temp tables, advisory locks, or LISTEN registrations are gone. The application only finds out later, indirectly, when something that depended on that state fails.

2. It is not possible to build a custom pool on top of the native client. Applications that need more advanced pooling than the built-in provides — custom acquisition and queueing policy, explicit health checks and eviction rules, connection retirement on a schedule the app controls, read-replica routing, per-tenant pools, or connection-level metrics — cannot layer that on Bun.SQL today, because the layer underneath silently reconnects and runs its own idle and lifetime timers that cannot be disabled, observed, or overridden. Any pool built on top would be managing connections whose identity and lifecycle it does not actually control.

Checking SQL.Options on 1.3.14, there is no reconnect, retry, or equivalent option, so there is currently no way to opt out.

