## Description

The `yaml` npm package supports a `lineWidth` option to control line wrapping of long scalar values in stringified output. `Bun.YAML.stringify()` has no equivalent — long strings are always emitted on a single line, with no way to configure wrapping.

This isn't broken behavior (output still parses back correctly), but it's a missing capability for anyone who wants human-readable, diff-friendly YAML output — e.g. config files or prompt templates meant to be reviewed by people, not just machines.

## Current behavior

```ts
import { YAML } from "bun";

YAML.stringify({ prompt: "word ".repeat(60) });
// -> single unbroken line, ~300+ chars wide

YAML.stringify({ prompt: "word ".repeat(60) }, null, 2);
// -> still a single unbroken line; indent doesn't affect wrapping
```

## Requested behavior

Support a `lineWidth`-style option so long scalar values can be folded/wrapped at a configurable width:

```ts
import { YAML } from "bun";

// yaml package equivalent:
// stringify(data, { lineWidth: 80 })

YAML.stringify({ prompt: "word ".repeat(60) }, { lineWidth: 80 });
// -> wrapped at ~80 chars per line, still valid YAML
```

## Why this matters

Not a blocker for parsing/round-tripping, but it closes a real gap for anyone using `Bun.YAML` to generate human-reviewed YAML files, and brings `Bun.YAML` closer to a true drop-in for the `yaml` package it's positioned to replace.

