## What happened?

`crypto.verify(algorithm, data, key, signature)` with `algorithm` `null` or `undefined` and an EC (P-256) public key throws (Bun spells it `error:06000077:...:NO_DEFAULT_DIGEST`; `e.code` is `ERR_OSSL_NO_DEFAULT_DIGEST` in the table below). Node (OpenSSL 3) infers SHA-256 as the default digest for EC keys in that call and returns `true`. RSA and Ed25519 keys behave the same in both runtimes; only EC-with-no-digest diverges.

This matters more than a thrown error usually would, because the Sigstore JavaScript libraries call `crypto.verify` exactly that way and wrap it in `try { ... } catch { return false }`: `@sigstore/core` (signature verification of Rekor's ECDSA-P256 inclusion promises) and `@tufjs/models` (the Sigstore TUF root, whose keys are all `ecdsa-sha2-nistp256`). Under Bun the throw becomes a silent `false`, so `@sigstore/verify` reports `inclusion promise could not be verified` and `@sigstore/tuf` reports `root was signed by 0/3 keys` for perfectly valid material. Nothing is unsafe (it fails closed), but any Bun program that verifies Sigstore bundles or TUF metadata refuses everything, with an error message that points at the data rather than the runtime.

