### What steps can reproduce the bug?

Run this JavaScript with Bun:

```js
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import net from "node:net";

const socket = new net.Socket();
let received;

const server = net.createServer(value => {
  received = value;
});

server.emit("connection", socket);
assert.strictEqual(received, socket);
```

### What is the expected behavior?

The callback passed to `net.createServer()` should be registered as a normal `"connection"` event listener, so `server.emit("connection", socket)` should call it with that socket.

The reproduction passes on Node 24 and Node 26. `server.listenerCount("connection")` is `1` on both.

### What do you see instead?

The callback is not called. `received` remains `undefined`, so the assertion fails. `server.listenerCount("connection")` is `0`.


### Additional information

This breaks the socket handoff used by [Mockttp after an HTTP CONNECT request](https://github.com/httptoolkit/mockttp/blob/v4.6.1/src/server/http-combo-server.ts#L396-L434). Mockttp emits the tunnel socket back into its combined server so it can detect the next protocol. On Bun, the server callback does not run and the post-CONNECT connection stalls.

## What happened

`Bun.listen()`'s `socket.write()` (server-side TCP, not `Bun.serve`/HTTP) has a
measured fixed per-call cost of **~4.4–4.6 µs** for a tiny (5-byte) payload on
an already-open, already-warm connection — roughly **10x** the cost of the
application-level work (RESP parse + dispatch) it's paired with in a
request/response server we benchmark against Redis/Valkey. We don't know if
this is expected/inherent to the write path, or something that could be
cheaper — filing as a performance report with a minimal, standalone repro in
case it's useful, not as a claim that anything is broken.

## Repro

Two files, no dependencies. Start the server, then run the client against it.

`server.ts`:
```ts
// Times ONLY the socket.write() call itself.
const REPLY = new TextEncoder().encode("+OK\r\n"); // 5 bytes
let totalNs = 0n;
let n = 0;

Bun.listen({
  hostname: "127.0.0.1",
  port: 7395,
  socket: {
    open(socket) {
      socket.setNoDelay(true);
    },
    data(socket, _chunk) {
      const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();
      socket.write(REPLY);
      const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();
      totalNs += t1 - t0;
      n++;
      if (n % 20_000 === 0) {
        console.error(`n=${n} avg socket.write()=${Number(totalNs) / n} ns`);
      }
    },
    close() {},
    error() {},
  },
});
console.error("listening on 127.0.0.1:7395");
```

`client.ts`:
```ts
// 8 concurrent connections, 12,500 unpipelined request/reply round trips
// each (100,000 total) — one write in flight per connection at a time.
const HOST = "127.0.0.1";
const PORT = 7395;
const CLIENTS = 8;
const REQUESTS_PER_CLIENT = 12_500;
const PING = new TextEncoder().encode("*1\r\n$4\r\nPING\r\n");

async function runClient(): Promise<void> {
  await new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {
    let received = 0;
    Bun.connect({
      hostname: HOST,
      port: PORT,
      socket: {
        open(socket) {
          socket.write(PING);
        },
        data(socket) {
          received++;
          if (received >= REQUESTS_PER_CLIENT) {
            socket.end();
            resolve();
            return;
          }
          socket.write(PING);
        },
        close() { resolve(); },
        error(_s, err) { reject(err); },
      },
    });
  });
}

const start = Date.now();
await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: CLIENTS }, () => runClient()));
console.log(`${CLIENTS * REQUESTS_PER_CLIENT} requests in ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
```

Run:
```
bun run server.ts &
bun run client.ts
```

##

## Summary

Bun's **two-argument** `import.meta.resolve(specifier, parent)` fails for bare package names, while the **one-argument** form succeeds. Node.js accepts both.

This breaks tooling that uses the Node-compatible two-arg form (e.g. [WXT](https://github.com/wxt-dev/wxt) `resolveWxtModuleDir`), so `bun --bun` / `bunx --bun wxt` crashes with `Cannot find package 'wxt'`.

Upstream workaround PR: https://github.com/wxt-dev/wxt/pull/2607 (switch callers to one-arg).

## Environment

- Bun 1.4.0 (Windows)
- Node 24.x (for comparison)

## Reproduction

In a project with `wxt` installed under `node_modules/wxt`:

```js
// save as node_modules/wxt/dist/core/probe.mjs and run with bun / node
try {
  console.log('one-arg', import.meta.resolve('wxt'));
} catch (e) {
  console.log('one-arg FAIL', e.message);
}
try {
  console.log('two-arg', import.meta.resolve('wxt', import.meta.url));
} catch (e) {
  console.log('two-arg FAIL', e.message);
}
```

**Bun:**
```
one-arg file:///…/node_modules/wxt/dist/index.mjs
two-arg FAIL Cannot find package 'wxt' imported from file:///…/node_modules/wxt/dist/core/probe.mjs
```

**Node:**
```
one-arg file:///…/node_modules/wxt/dist/index.mjs
two-arg file:///…/node_modules/wxt/dist/index.mjs
```

Also fails for any parent URL (existing or synthetic), e.g. resolving `@wxt-dev/unocss` with `parent = pathToFileURL(projectRoot + '/index.js').href`.

## Expected

Two-arg `import.meta.resolve(specifier, parent)` should resolve bare package names the same way Node does (including self-resolution from a file inside that package).

## Impact

