### What happens

`session.altsvc(alt, { origin })` parses the `origin` property with `new URL()` before it writes the ALTSVC frame. Node writes the property unchanged.

Repro. A raw HTTP/2 client prints the origin of each ALTSVC frame that the server sends:

```js
server.on("session", session => {
  for (const origin of ["https://example.org:8111/path", "not a url", "https://caf\xe9.example"]) {
    try {
      session.altsvc('h2=":8000"', { origin });
    } catch (err) {
      console.log(origin, err.code);
    }
  }
});
```

| `origin` property | Node v26.3.0 sends | Bun 1.4.1 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `"https://example.org:8111/path"` | `https://example.org:8111/path` | sends `https://example.org:8111` |
| `"not a url"` | `not a url` | throws `ERR_HTTP2_ALTSVC_INVALID_ORIGIN` |
| `"https://caf\xe9.example"` | `https://caf\xe9.example` | sends `https://xn--caf-dma.example` |

A string or a `URL` argument gives the same result in both runtimes.

### Cause

In the object branch, `ServerHttp2Session.altsvc()` calls `getOrigin(origin, true)` (`src/js/node/http2.ts:4397`). `getOrigin` parses a string with `new URL()`.

Node rejects only `"null"` and the empty string in that branch. The comment there says that Node does not verify an object origin, and that the user must serialize it:
https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/internal/http2/core.js#L1738-L1752

