### Why

- In node `bytesRead` is a getter on `Socket.prototype`. With a handle it returns `handle.bytesRead`. A new connection has a new handle, so the count starts at `0`. `_destroy()` saves the last value for reads after the close ([net.js#L881-L882](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/net.js#L881-L882), [net.js#L948-L950](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/net.js#L948-L950)).
- In bun `bytesRead` is an own data property. The `Socket` constructor sets it to `0` (`this.bytesRead = 0` in `src/js/node/net.ts`) and the `data` handlers add to it. Nothing sets it back when `connect()` starts a new connection.

### What a fix needs

- Set the count back to `0` when a new connection starts, at the place that matches node's `initSocketHandle`.
- Node also differs in shape: `bytesRead` is an accessor on the prototype with no setter, and `Object.keys(socket)` does not list it. That part is optional.
- A test in `test/js/node/net/socket-reconnect-live.test.ts`.

