### What happens

`worker.getHeapSnapshot()`, `worker.startCpuProfile()` and the `stop()` of the handle it returns (node:worker_threads) stay pending while the worker runs synchronous JavaScript. They settle only when the worker returns to its event loop. With `while (true) {}` in the worker they never settle, and `terminate()` then rejects them with `ERR_WORKER_NOT_RUNNING`. Node.js settles them while the worker is busy.

These are the calls a parent uses to find out why a worker is stuck, so they are least available when they are wanted.

```js
import { Worker } from "node:worker_threads";
const what = process.argv[2];
const w = new Worker("const t=Date.now(); while(Date.now()-t<6000){}", { eval: true });
await new Promise(r => w.once("online", r));
await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 300));
const t0 = performance.now();
if (what === "snapshot") (await w.getHeapSnapshot()).resume();
else {
  const handle = await w.startCpuProfile();
  console.log("startCpuProfile", Math.round(performance.now() - t0), "ms");
  await handle.stop();
}
console.log(what, Math.round(performance.now() - t0), "ms");
await w.terminate();
```

