| --- | --- | --- |
| `getHeapSnapshot()` | 98 ms | 5738 ms |
| `startCpuProfile()` | 26 ms | 5714 ms |
| `handle.stop()` | 3 ms | after the job ends |

The Bun numbers are the rest of the worker's 6 s job. The same happens when the synchronous job runs inside a `parentPort` message handler.

`worker.cpuUsage()` and `worker.getHeapStatistics()` had the same problem. They do not need the worker's cooperation. #42360 answers them on the parent thread.

### Cause

Each call registers a promise on the parent and posts a task to the worker with `WorkerMessagingProxy::postTaskToWorkerGlobalScope` (`src/jsc/bindings/webcore/JSWorker.cpp`: `getHeapSnapshot`, `startCpuProfileInternal`, `stopCpuProfileInternal`). That is an ordinary task on the worker's event loop. While the worker's entry module still evaluates, the proxy is `Pending` and only appends the task to `m_pendingTasks`, so it is not on the worker's loop at all. Nothing interrupts the worker's VM.

Node.js uses `Environment::RequestInterrupt`, which queues a native callback and triggers both `v8::Isolate::RequestInterrupt` and `uv_async_send`. Whichever reaches the worker first runs it.

