### Why

- In node, `pause()` on an `onread` socket stops the handle (`handle.reading = false`, `readStop()`). `read()` starts it again through `tryReadStart` ([net.js#L817-L827](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/net.js#L817-L827), [net.js#L839-L845](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/net.js#L839-L845)). After the callback, `onStreamRead` stops the handle only when the callback returned `false` ([stream_base_commons.js#L179](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/internal/stream_base_commons.js#L179), [#L191-L198](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/internal/stream_base_commons.js#L191-L198)).
- In bun, the `deliver` closure in the `Socket` constructor (`src/js/node/net.ts`) checks `ret === false || self.isPaused()` after each callback. `read()` does not clear the paused state of the Readable, so `isPaused()` is still `true`. `deliver` keeps the rest of the chunk as the undelivered tail and stops the handle.
- The `read()` call ran inside the callback, before the tail existed, so `drainOnreadTail()` had nothing to schedule. Nothing delivers the tail until the next `resume()` or `read()`.

### What a fix needs

- The stop condition after the callback must follow the state that `pause()`, `read()` and `resume()` set on the handle. The `isPaused()` state of the Readable is not that state.
- A test next to the other `onread` tests in `test/js/node/net/node-net.test.ts`.

