### Why

- Node's `TLSSocket` constructor builds the options for `net.Socket` itself. It passes `onread: !socket ? tlsOptions.onread : null` ([wrap.js#L590-L601](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/internal/tls/wrap.js#L590-L601)). So `onread` applies only when the TLS socket dials its own connection.
- Bun's `TLSSocket` passes the caller's options object on to the `net.Socket` constructor (`src/js/node/tls.ts:757-762`). It removes only `readable` and `writable`. So `onread` reaches `net.Socket` for a wrapped socket too.

### What a fix needs

- Do not pass `onread` to `net.Socket` when the TLS socket wraps a stream (`options.socket`, or the first argument of `new TLSSocket(socket, ...)`).
- Node passes a fixed set of options to `net.Socket` (`handle`, `allowHalfOpen`, `pauseOnCreate`, `manualStart`, `highWaterMark`, `onread`, `signal`, `noDelay`, `keepAlive`, `keepAliveInitialDelay`). A wider fix builds that set and drops the rest. Check each option that bun's `Socket` constructor reads before you drop it.
- A test in `test/js/node/tls/node-tls-upgrade.test.ts`.

