## Platform

Windows 10 22H2 x64, build 19045, local filesystem. The same operation sequence did not reproduce on macOS ARM64 with either version.

## Summary

On Windows 1.4.2, a single `FileSink.write()` returns a number and the immediately following `flush()` returns `0`, while the submitted write may still be in flight. Awaiting both return values can be followed by an empty read of the file. The content becomes readable later.

On Windows 1.3.14, the same sequence returned Promises and the reads succeeded. This affects a live command-output transcript: the writer needs to remain open for more output, but another consumer needs to read the bytes already submitted.

## Reduced reproduction

The following reduces the write/flush/read sequence used in the tests below. Run with `bun repro.ts`. The exact failure frequency is timing-dependent; log results after the loop to avoid changing the timing of each read.

```ts
import { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";

const root = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "bun-filesink-flush-"));
const iterations = 300;
let failures = 0;
const samples: unknown[] = [];

try {
  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    const path = join(root, `${i}.log`);
    const writer = Bun.file(path).writer();
    try {
      const writeResult = writer.write("first\n");
      const flushResult = writer.flush();
      await writeResult;
      await flushResult;

      const actual = readFileSync(path, "utf8");
      if (actual !== "first\n") {
        failures++;
        if (samples.length < 5) samples.push({
          i, actual,
          write: writeResult instanceof Promise ? "Promise" : writeResult,
          flush: flushResult instanceof Promise ? "Promise" : flushResult,
        });
      }
    } finally {
      await writer.end();
      rmSync(path);
    }
  }
} finally {
  rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
}

console.log({
  version: Bun.version, revision: Bun.revision,
  platform: process.platform, arch: process.arch,
  iterations, failures, samples,
});
```

