### Why

- In node `_bytesDispatched` reads the handle: `this._handle ? this._handle.bytesWritten : this[kBytesWritten]` ([lib/net.js](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/net.js#L1043-L1045)). `bytesWritten` adds the bytes that are still queued in JS.
- In bun `_bytesDispatched` and `bytesWritten` read `this[kBytesWritten]` only (`src/js/node/net.ts`). The JS write path copies the native count into it after each write. The h2 frame writer writes through the native socket and never runs that path.
- `src/js/node/http2.ts` hides this for `session.socket`: both socket proxies special-case `bytesWritten` with `socketBytesWritten()`, which takes the larger of the native count and the JS count. A socket that the user holds does not go through a proxy.

### What a fix needs

- `_bytesDispatched` reads `this._handle.bytesWritten` when there is a handle, like node. `_destroy` already saves the last value in `kBytesWritten`.
- Then the two `bytesWritten` cases in the h2 socket proxies can go.
- One related difference: when `connect()` replaces a live connection, the native wrapper is reused and keeps its written count, so `bytesWritten` continues from the old connection. #42313 resets the read count for that case (`detach_for_reconnect`), not the written count.

This is the write-side twin of #42302. #42313 fixes the read side and leaves this out on purpose.

