### What steps can reproduce the bug?

`db.query()` caches the compiled statement, and the cache holds `Database.MAX_QUERY_CACHE_SIZE` (20) entries. The documentation notes that evicted statements keep working — but an evicted statement is still owned by the connection, so it is only finalized when the `Database` closes.

The consequence is that once a process uses **more distinct SQL texts than the cache has slots**, nearly every execution evicts a neighbour, compiles a fresh statement, and pins it for the life of the connection. Memory then grows with the number of **executions**, not with the number of distinct queries — so it keeps growing forever in a long-running process, even though the set of queries is small and fixed.

An easy way to end up above 20 texts without meaning to is a multi-row `INSERT`, whose text changes with the row count.

```ts
// repro.ts — bun repro.ts [executions] [distinctTexts] [cacheSize]
import { Database } from "bun:sqlite";

const EXECUTIONS = Number(process.argv[2] ?? 40_000);
const DISTINCT_TEXTS = Number(process.argv[3] ?? 40);
const CACHE = Number(process.argv[4] ?? 0);
if (CACHE > 0) Database.MAX_QUERY_CACHE_SIZE = CACHE;

const db = new Database(":memory:");
db.run("CREATE TABLE t (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, v INTEGER)");

const sql = (rows: number) =>
  `INSERT INTO t VALUES ${Array.from({ length: rows }, () => "(?, ?)").join(", ")} ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`;

let seq = 0;
Bun.gc(true);
const before = process.memoryUsage().rss;

for (let i = 0; i < EXECUTIONS; i++) {
  const rows = 1 + (i % DISTINCT_TEXTS);
  // ids cycle over 1000, so the table never grows and nothing here is data
  const params = Array.from({ length: rows }, () => {
    const id = seq++ % 1000;
    return [`id-${id}`, id];
  }).flat();
  db.query(sql(rows)).run(...params);
}

Bun.gc(true);
const retained = Math.round((process.memoryUsage().rss - before) / 1e6);
const rows = (db.query("SELECT count(*) AS n FROM t").get() as { n: number }).n;
console.log(`executions=${EXECUTIONS} distinctTexts=${DISTINCT_TEXTS} cache=${CACHE || 20} -> retained ${retained} MB (table: ${rows} rows)`);
db.close();
```

The table is pinned at 1000 rows throughout, so none of the retained memory is data.

