### What happens

At a natural exit of the main thread or of a Worker, `NapiEnv::cleanup()` runs the cleanup hooks and the remaining finalizers of each addon env. Node runs this phase with `can_call_into_js()` false. Every Node-API function that starts with `NAPI_PREAMBLE` returns `napi_cannot_run_js` (module version 10 or later) or `napi_pending_exception` (older) there and does nothing.

Bun's `NAPI_PREAMBLE` (`src/jsc/bindings/napi.cpp:93`) refuses only when `isStoppingOrStopped()` is true. That is false at a natural exit. So these calls run, and a teardown finalizer can call into JS. A Worker that `terminate()` stops is already refused, as in Node.

This is a design question and not a one-line fix. Addons that work in Bun today can depend on the calls that run in this phase.

### Consequence 1: an addon is re-entered after its instance data is finalized

Bun tears the envs down one after the other. JS that a finalizer of a later env calls can call a function of an addon whose env is already torn down. The `napi_set_instance_data` finalizer of that addon has already run, and `napi_get_instance_data` still returns the old pointer. An addon that frees its instance data in that finalizer (`Napi::Env::SetInstanceData` does) then reads freed memory.

