`fs.globSync`, `fs.glob` and `fs.promises.glob` can return fewer paths for a pattern array (or a brace group) than the union of the results of each pattern. Node.js returns the same results, because `src/js/internal/fs/glob.ts` is a port of its JS glob walker. #42870 fixes the largest cause (an early `return` in the loop over directory children). This issue records what remains after it.

### Repros

Results are sorted. "Expected" is the union of the results of the single patterns.

1. Fixture: `d/x/x/f1`, `d/x/f2`.

   ```js
   fs.globSync(["**/x/*", "./d/x/x/*", "d/x/*"], { cwd });
   // Bun 1.4.3-canary, #42870, Node.js v26.8.2: [ "d/x/x/f1" ]
   // Expected: [ "d/x/f2", "d/x/x", "d/x/x/f1" ]. Bun 1.3.14 returned all three.
   ```

2. Fixture: `x/y/g`, `x/y/z/y/f`.

   ```js
   fs.globSync(["x/**/y/*", "./x/y/**/y/*", "x/y/*"], { cwd });
   // #42870, Node.js v26.8.2: [ "x/y/z/y/f" ]
   // Expected, and Bun 1.4.3-canary: [ "x/y/g", "x/y/z", "x/y/z/y/f" ]
   ```

3. Fixture: `a/b/`, `a/x`, `a/z`.

   ```js
   fs.globSync("a/**/../{x,z}", { cwd });
   // Bun 1.4.3-canary, #42870, Node.js v26.8.2: [ "a/x" ]
   // Expected: [ "a/x", "a/z" ]
   ```

The Node.js v27 nightly of 2026-09-15, which has the native glob engine, returns the same results as v26.8.2 for all three.

### Causes

All three are in `src/js/internal/fs/glob.ts`.

- `Cache.add` adds every suffix of a pattern to the seen cache. Then it reports "seen" if one of them was there before, and the caller skips the whole traversal. The suffixes that were new are now marked, but nothing walked them. A later pattern with the same suffix is skipped too (repros 1 and 2). Repro 2 worked before #42870 only because the early `return` stopped the walker before it queued the merged pattern.
- The `next === ".."` branch queues its continuation with `#subpatterns.set`, and only if no other pattern queued that path in the same step. The continuation of the second pattern is dropped (repro 3).
- The shortcut for a single literal segment calls `setDirentName` on the Dirent from the stat cache. The readdir cache holds the same object. For a literal `..`, this renames the entry of the parent directory to `..`. This is visible with `withFileTypes: true`: over `a/b/c/d` and `a/x`, `a/*/**/../../*` returns a Dirent with the name `..` for the path `a` (Node.js v26.8.2 does the same). A later listing of that directory also does not match the renamed entry. With a fix for `Cache.add` alone, that second effect drops paths.

