process.send("hello");
```

`bun parent.js` on Linux (1.4.3-canary.1+09bb54630):

```
child: process.send is a function
parent got hello
```

On Windows Server 2019 x64 (1.4.3-canary.1+f5649a7ed):

```
child: process.send is a function
parent: no message after 5 s
```

`Bun.spawn({ cmd: [process.execPath, "--watch", "child.js"], ipc() {} })` behaves the same way.

### Cause

POSIX reloads with `execve`, which keeps fd 3. Windows cannot, so `bun --watch` first becomes a "watcher manager" (`become_watcher_manager`, `src/sys/windows/mod.rs`). The manager spawns the real process with `CreateProcessW` and spawns it again each time it exits with the reload exit code.

- `spawn_watcher_child` passes the three standard handles only (`cbReserved2: 0, lpReserved2: null`). The real process has no fd 3.
- `uv_disable_stdio_inheritance()` runs at the start of every bun process. In the manager it clears the inherit flag of the IPC pipe handle. `windows_enable_stdio_inheritance()` sets it again for stdin, stdout and stderr only.
- `NODE_CHANNEL_FD=3` still reaches the real process through the environment, so `process.send` exists there.

### Scope

- Other extra stdio pipes (fd 3 and up) are lost the same way.
- `test/cli/watch/watch.test.ts` › "IPC to the parent survives a --watch reload" is skipped on Windows.
- To forward the pipe through the manager is not a full fix. A socket that is sent over IPC is duplicated for the pid of the direct child (`WSADuplicateSocketW`). With a manager in between, that pid is the manager, not the process that reads the message.

