### What happens

A `net.Socket` that adopts an fd (`socket.connect({ fd })`) emits `'error'` two times when the flush of a buffered write fails with a fatal `send()` errno. Node emits it one time.

Each connection that a `node:cluster` worker accepts is such a socket. `onClusterConnection` in `src/js/node/net.ts` calls `socket.connect({ fd: clientHandle.fd, fdIsRawSocket: true })`, and that path uses the `SocketHandlers` table.

### Cause

`SocketHandlers.error` (`src/js/node/net.ts:538`) fails the parked write callback with the error. The Writable then destroys the stream and queues `'error'`. After that, the handler also calls `self.emit("error", error)`.

The other two handler tables emit one time. `ServerHandlers.error` (plain TCP branch) calls `destroy(error)` only if the socket is not destroyed. `SocketHandlers2.error` defers `destroy(error)` to the next tick.

The native caller is the fatal-flush path of `on_writable` in `src/runtime/socket/socket_body.rs` (POSIX only). #43026 adds the tail of `on_open` as a second caller of the same handler. It does not change the handler.

