### What steps can reproduce the bug?

Repeatedly requiring an already-cached CommonJS module adds another reference to Bun's native `JSCommonJSModule::m_children` vector whenever the JavaScript `module.children` value has not yet been materialized.

Repeated cached `require()` calls are expected CommonJS behavior. After the first load, calls resolving to the same filename should return the cached exports without re-evaluating the module or adding duplicate edges to the module graph.

Create `child.cjs`:

```js
module.exports = { value: 1 };
```

Create `repro.cjs`:

```js
const { performance } = require("node:perf_hooks");

function gcMs() {
  const start = performance.now();

  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    Bun.gc(true);
  }

  return Number(((performance.now() - start) / 10).toFixed(3));
}

console.log({
  stage: "before",
  gcMs: gcMs(),
});

for (let batch = 1; batch <= 5; batch++) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    require("./child.cjs");
  }

  console.log({
    requires: batch * 1_000_000,
    gcMs: gcMs(),
  });
}

// Do not read module.children before this point. Reading it materializes and
// deduplicates the JavaScript array, which clears the accumulated native vector.
console.log({
  stage: "after reading module.children",
  uniqueChildren: module.children.length,
  gcMs: gcMs(),
});
```

Run:

```sh
bun repro.cjs
```

### What is the expected behavior?

Repeatedly requiring the same cached module should not create millions of duplicate references to the same child module.

The parent has one unique child, so the representation scanned by the garbage collector should remain bounded and full-GC time should remain approximately flat.

This is part of the normal CommonJS caching contract, not an unusual application pattern. Node documents that modules are cached after their first load and that repeated calls resolving to the same filename return the same object: https://nodejs.org/api/modules.html#caching

As a comparison, Node.js 24.13.0 kept `module.children.length === 1`, with mean forced-GC time remaining between approximately 1.2 and 1.6 ms throughout five million cached requires.

