### What happens

On Linux, `getPeakRSS()` (`src/jsc/bindings/BunProcess.cpp`) returns `getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss`. That value survives exec. At exec the kernel folds the peak of the address space that the process leaves into the new image (`exec_mmap` calls `setmax_mm_hiwater_rss`). A bun process that a large parent starts with vfork or `posix_spawn` reports the peak of the parent as its own peak.

`getPeakRSS()` feeds these values:

- `memoryUsage().peak` in `bun:jsc`
- the peak memory line of the crash report (`bun_sys::self_process_peak_memory_usage`)
- `resourceUsage.maxRss` in `process.report.getReport()`

### Reproduction

```js
// peak-probe.js
const big = process.argv[2] === "big" ? Buffer.alloc(700 * 1024 * 1024, 1) : null;
const child = `
  const { memoryUsage } = require("bun:jsc");
  const hwm = Number(require("fs").readFileSync("/proc/self/status", "utf8").match(/VmHWM:\\s+(\\d+) kB/)[1]) * 1024;
  const mb = n => Math.round(n / 1048576);
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ jscPeakMB: mb(memoryUsage().peak), vmHwmMB: mb(hwm), currentRssMB: mb(process.memoryUsage.rss()) }));
`;
const proc = Bun.spawn({ cmd: [process.execPath, "-e", child], stdout: "pipe", stderr: "inherit" });
console.log(process.argv[2] ?? "small", (await proc.stdout.text()).trim(), big?.length ?? "");
```

Linux x64, bun 1.4.3-canary:

```
$ bun peak-probe.js small
small {"jscPeakMB":33,"vmHwmMB":32,"currentRssMB":33}
$ bun peak-probe.js big
big {"jscPeakMB":728,"vmHwmMB":32,"currentRssMB":33} 734003200
```

The child never uses more than 33 MB, but it reports a peak of 728 MB because its parent holds a 700 MB buffer.

### Expected

These values show the peak of this process. `VmHWM` in `/proc/self/status` is the high-water mark of the current address space. It starts at zero after exec, so it does not have this problem.

`process.resourceUsage().maxRSS` is `ru_maxrss` in Node.js too, so that API can stay as it is.

