handler B: stream.respond({}); stream.pushStream(...); stream.end();
handler C: stream.respond({}); stream.end("body"); stream.pushStream(...);

node A, B, C: HEADERS(1) PUSH_PROMISE(1) HEADERS(2) DATA(1,END_STREAM) DATA(2,END_STREAM)
bun A:        HEADERS(1) DATA(1,END_STREAM) PUSH_PROMISE(1) HEADERS(2) DATA(2,END_STREAM)
bun B:        HEADERS(1) PUSH_PROMISE(1) DATA(1,END_STREAM) HEADERS(2) DATA(2,END_STREAM)
bun C:        HEADERS(1) DATA(1,END_STREAM) PUSH_PROMISE(1) HEADERS(2) DATA(2,END_STREAM)
```

With handler A, a Bun server and a node client give the client events `["req response 200","req end"]`. A Bun client surfaces the push, because the Bun client accepts a PUSH_PROMISE on a parent that is already closed (see below).

### Why

nghttp2 queues frames and serializes them when the session flushes. HEADERS and PUSH_PROMISE leave before pending DATA. In Bun, `Http2Stream._final` in `src/js/node/http2.ts` calls `native.writeStream(id, "", "ascii", true, cb)` and then `native.flush()` at once, and the last `_write` puts END_STREAM on its DATA frame. `pushStream` then calls `parser.pushPromise(this.id, ...)` (`push_promise` in `src/runtime/api/bun/h2_frame_parser.rs`), which does not look at the state of the parent.

