### What happens

A `node:http` server upgrades a request with the builtin `ws` (`handleUpgrade()`), and the Upgrade request declares a body (`Content-Length: 17`). `server.upgrade()` moves the socket to the native WebSocket at once. After that, no byte of the request body can reach `req`. Node v26.3.0 delivers the body through `req` first, and WebSocket frames start after the body.

Found while reviewing #43427. That PR only fixes a keep-alive leak on this path and does not change any of the behaviour below. It is the same on bun 1.4.3.

### Measured

Raw TCP client, `handleUpgrade()` inside the `'upgrade'` event and from `setImmediate`, a reader of `req` attached right after `handleUpgrade()`. Node v26.3.0 with `ws` 8.18.3, and bun 1.4.3.

| Body | Node v26.3.0 | Bun |
| --- | --- | --- |
| never sent | `'upgrade'` gets an `UpgradeStream`, `req.complete === false`, `req` stays open. The next bytes on the wire are read as the body. | `req` stays open with `complete === false` (same). The next bytes go to the WebSocket as frames. |
| sent after the 101 | `req` gets `'data'`, `'end'`, `'close'`, `complete === true`. A frame sent after it reaches `ws`. | `req` gets nothing. The body bytes reach the WebSocket parser, and the later frame is not delivered. |
| sent with the head | The body is complete before `'upgrade'`: plain `Socket`, `req.complete === true`, `req` emits data/end/close. | `'upgrade'` fires before the body is parsed. An upgrade inside the event drops the body. An upgrade from a later task delivers it. |

