### What happens

On Linux, a child whose output is not streamed writes its stdout and stderr to a memfd. bun reads the memfd once, after the child has exited, in `PosixBufferedReader::final_buffer` (`src/io/PipeReader.rs`):

```rust
let result = sys::File::borrow(&fd)
    .read_to_end_with_array_list(&mut self._buffer, sys::SizeHint::UnknownSize);
self.handle.close(None, None::<fn(*mut c_void)>);
if let Err(err) = result {
    bun_core::debug_warn!("error reading from memfd\n{}", err);
    return self.buffer();
}
```

When that read fails, a release build prints nothing. The caller gets the bytes that were read before the error and treats them as the whole output.

The callers that spawn with a memfd are the lifecycle script runner (`src/install/lifecycle_script_runner.rs`) and the git runner (`src/install/git_runner.rs`). The lifecycle runner shows the output of a script that failed, so that output can be cut short with no notice. The git runner uses the output of git.

### Status

This comes from the code. It is not reproduced: it needs a read of the memfd to fail.

A failed read of a socket or a pipe takes a different path (`on_error`, then `on_reader_error` of the parent), and each parent reports it. Only the memfd path drops the error.

### What to do

Make `final_buffer` return the error, for example as `sys::Result<&mut Vec<u8>>`, and let each caller report it the way it reports a failed read of a socket.

### What happens

On a `node:http` server, the socket that `'connect'` and `'upgrade'` listeners get loses data when the connection closes while the socket still holds unread bytes. The socket emits `'close'` with no `'end'`, the unread bytes are dropped, and `stream.pipeline()` never calls back. Node v26.3.0 delivers every byte, emits `'end'`, and then closes.

The reader must be behind at the close: `pipe()` or `pipeline()` to a slower destination, `for await`, or `pause()` and a later `resume()`. A `'data'` listener that takes every chunk at once does not show it.

