### What happens

On a `node:http` server, every request whose read holds bytes behind the request head copies those bytes into a new `Buffer`. Only the `'connect'` event and the `'upgrade'` event of a request without a body pass that `Buffer` to user code, as `head`. For every other request JavaScript drops it.

For an upload, the bytes behind the head are the part of the body that came in the same read. The body is then copied one more time for the request stream.

