```
no workers, end(1 MiB): 163 bytes, round trip: true
1 worker, end(1 MiB): 0 bytes, round trip: false
1 worker, write(1 MiB) + end(): 0 bytes, round trip: false
1 worker, write(512 KiB) + end(512 KiB): 168 bytes, round trip: true
```

Node v26.10.0 prints the same sizes. Its `zstdDecompressSync` throws `Z_BUF_ERROR` for the empty input.

### Cause

With workers, `ZSTD_compressStream2(..., ZSTD_e_continue)` takes at most one job of input in a call. It returns with input left and with space left in the output. zstd documents that: `ZSTD_e_continue` only has to make some progress.

The write callback in `src/js/node/zlib.ts` reads "input left, and output space left" as the end of the stream:

https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/blob/542f52be3d60426c83e817280c62f8433c420bda/src/js/node/zlib.ts#L554-L562

It calls `self.push(null)` and drops the rest of the chunk. That rule is correct for a decompressor that reached the end of its frame. Node has the same code in `lib/zlib.js`.

### What happens

The zstd classes of `node:zlib` ignore a `dictionary` option that is not a `Buffer`, `TypedArray` or `DataView`. Node v26.10.0 uses an `ArrayBuffer` dictionary and throws `ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE` for each other type.

bun matches Node v26.3.0 here. Node changed two times after that:

- v26.7.0 accepts an `ArrayBuffer` ([nodejs/node#64599](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/64599), "zlib: accept ArrayBuffer dictionary in Zstd").
- v26.10.0 throws for the other types (commit [`5e55085bc1`](https://github.com/nodejs/node/commit/5e55085bc1), "zlib: reject invalid zstd dictionaries", from [nodejs/node#65867](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/65867)).

