### What happens

A zstd compressor stream with `ZSTD_c_nbWorkers >= 1` compresses on threads that zstd owns. `destroy()`, `close()`, `reset()`, a second `_handle.init()` and the finalizer free the zstd context on the JS thread. `ZSTD_freeCCtx` joins the worker threads, and a worker finishes the job that it runs before it exits. So the call blocks the JS thread until that job ends.

Node v26.3.0 blocks for the same time, so this is not a difference from Node.

### Numbers

One worker, the default job size, one job of text that compresses badly, and the call right after the write callback, so almost the whole job is left. Release builds on linux x64, on a loaded machine. The process CPU time of the call is within a few percent of the wall time in each row.

| Level | Job | `destroy()` blocks, bun main 4b02e1031d | Node v26.3.0 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 3 | 8 MiB | 51 to 176 ms | 83 to 243 ms |
| 9 | 32 MiB | 474 to 1042 ms | 480 to 1034 ms |
| 19 | 32 MiB | 54.8 s | 58.3 s |

`close()`, `reset()` and a second `_handle.init()` block in the same way (82 to 311 ms at level 3).

### Reproduction

```js
// bun stall.cjs 9
const zlib = require("node:zlib");
const { pbkdf2Sync } = require("node:crypto");
const C = zlib.constants;
const level = Number(process.argv[2] || 3);
// The default job is 4 windows: 8 MiB at level 3, 32 MiB at levels 9 and 19.
const jobBytes = (level === 3 ? 8 : 32) * 1024 * 1024;
const input = Buffer.from(pbkdf2Sync("input", "", 1, jobBytes / 2, "sha256").toString("hex"));
const stream = zlib
  .createZstdCompress({ params: { [C.ZSTD_c_nbWorkers]: 1, [C.ZSTD_c_compressionLevel]: level } })
  .on("data", () => {});
stream.write(input, () => {
  const start = performance.now();
  stream.destroy();
  console.log(`level ${level}: destroy() blocked ${Math.round(performance.now() - start)} ms`);
});
```

### Cause

`Context::deinit_state` in `src/runtime/node/zlib/NativeZstd.rs` calls `ZSTD_freeCCtx` on the JS thread. With workers, `ZSTD_freeCCtx` calls `ZSTDMT_freeCCtx`, which joins the threads (`POOL_free`). zstd has no call that stops a job early.

### A possible fix

Free a compressor context that has workers off the JS thread, so that the join happens there. Points to decide:

- which thread waits: a work pool thread is then busy for the rest of the job, and a thread of its own costs a spawn per teardown
- what process exit does while such a free is pending
- how the leak checker lane sees the free

