### What happens

A `node:http2` client emits `'response'`, `'push'`, `'headers'`, `'trailers'` and the session `'stream'` event from inside the native frame dispatch. node emits them with `process.nextTick` ([core.js](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/internal/http2/core.js#L429), [core.js](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.3.0/lib/internal/http2/core.js#L456-L459)).

On a TCP or TLS socket the two give the same order, because both runtimes run the tick queue between two frames of one read. Over a JS transport (`http2.connect(url, { createConnection })` with a stream that is not a socket) they differ. The client parses a whole chunk inside one JS call, so no tick runs between its frames. node's listeners then run after the whole chunk. Bun's listeners run in the middle of it.

A raw server on the other end of a `duplexPair()` writes one chunk: SETTINGS, SETTINGS ACK, the response HEADERS on stream 1, and DATA "abc" on stream 1. The `'response'` listener prints `req.readableLength`.

```
node v26.3.0:                  3   (the DATA of the same chunk is already in the readable)
bun 1.4.2, 1.4.3-canary.1:     0   (the listener runs before the DATA frame is parsed)
```

