The same loop on Node 24 (`node --expose-gc`, `gc()` instead of `Bun.gc(true)`) prints `0/40`.

### What is the expected behavior?

`error.stack` starts with `Error: boom N` and keeps the function names, whenever it is first read.

### What do you see instead?

If a GC runs before the first read of `error.stack`, the header is just `Error` (no message) and function names become `<anonymous>`.

### Additional information

Our reading of the source (tag `bun-v1.4.2`, `src/jsc/bindings/FormatStackTraceForJS.cpp`), not verified with a debugger: `Error.stack` is materialized lazily. When the error is still unread at GC time and a captured frame's callee has been collected, `ErrorInstance::finalizeUnconditionally` materializes the stack via `computeErrorInfoWrapperToString` → `computeErrorInfoToString`, which calls the formatter with `errorInstance = nullptr`. That yields the bare name with an empty message, and function names resolved in `MustNotTriggerGC` mode (`<anonymous>`).

In practice this showed up as a rare (~0.1%) test flake in a suite that asserts on `error.stack` of errors thrown from short-lived async callbacks. Reading `error.stack` right after construction avoids it.

