## Environment

Bun 1.4.3 (`c6da4a4d3`), official `bun-darwin-aarch64.zip`; macOS 27.0 arm64. Also observed with Bun 1.4.2. No third-party dependencies, database, network, or service is required.

## Reproduction

Save as `nested-exit.test.ts`, then run `bun test --timeout=5000 nested-exit.test.ts`:

```ts
import { expect, test } from 'bun:test';
import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';
test('all exit events survive a nested tick', async () => {
  let nested = false, callbacks = 0, delivered = 0;
  const children = Array.from({ length: 20 }, () => Bun.spawn(['true'], {
    stdio: ['ignore', 'ignore', 'ignore'],
    onExit() {
      callbacks++;
      if (!nested) {
        nested = true;
        void expect(Bun.sleep(1)).resolves.toBeUndefined();
      }
    },
  }));
  for (const child of children) void child.exited.then(() => delivered++);
  await Bun.sleep(1000);
  const states = execFileSync('ps', ['-o', 'stat=', '-p', children.map(c => c.pid).join(',')], { encoding: 'utf8' }).trim().split('\n').map(s => s.trim());
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ callbacks, delivered, zombies: states.filter(s => s.startsWith('Z')).length }));
  for (const child of children) child.unref();
  expect(delivered).toBe(20);
});
```

## Actual

One isolated 1.4.3 run reported `{"callbacks":6,"delivered":6,"zombies":14}` and failed `Expected: 20, Received: 6`. Another diagnostic using the same nested-tick shape reported 1 delivered exit and 19 children in OS `Z` state. The count can vary with poll batching. These are exited children, not slow work: OS-level `ps` reports the undelivered children as defunct, while their `exitCode` remains null and `child.exited` never resolves.

## Expected

All 20 exit events/promises are eventually delivered and the children are reaped. The nested expectation should not strand the remaining ready process-exit events.

## Context

This blocks the cleanup in GBrain's `test/bounded-child-exec.test.ts` on Darwin even when all 20 calls under test have already completed. The Linux-specific fix in #30301 explicitly leaves macOS on `EV_ONESHOT`; this report is the Darwin case and does not claim that the separate `spawnSync` poll-drift fix is absent from 1.4.3.

The test deliberately keeps the nested `expect(...).resolves` trigger. It does not replace missing exit delivery with a timeout success or silently skip Darwin.

## Environment

Bun 1.4.3 (`c6da4a4d3`), official Darwin arm64 binary; macOS 27.0 arm64. Also observed with Bun 1.4.2. The same script was run with Node v26.7.0 on the same host for comparison.

## Reproduction

Save as `destroy-reader.mjs`, run `bun destroy-reader.mjs`, then `node destroy-reader.mjs`. All children and temporary files are owned and cleaned by this script. No network/database/service is required.

```js
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import { join } from 'node:path';
const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'bun-destroy-reader-'));
const marker = join(dir, 'ticks');
const entry = join(dir, 'writer.mjs');
writeFileSync(marker, '');
writeFileSync(entry, `import { appendFileSync } from 'node:fs';
process.on('SIGPIPE', () => process.exit(141));
for (const stream of [process.stdout, process.stderr]) stream.on('error', e => { if (e.code === 'EPIPE') process.exit(0); });
setInterval(() => { console.log('out'); console.error('err'); appendFileSync(${JSON.stringify(marker)}, 'x'); }, 50);
`);
const child = spawn(process.execPath, [entry], { stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'] });
let exited = 'alive';
child.on('exit', (code, signal) => { exited = code ?? signal; });
try {
  for (let n = 0; n < 100 && readFileSync(marker).length < 5; n++) await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 50));
  child.stdout.destroy(); child.stderr.destroy();
  const before = readFileSync(marker).length;
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1500));
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ destroyed: child.stdout.destroyed && child.stderr.destroyed, exit: exited, ticksAfterClose: readFileSync(marker).length - before }));
} finally {
  child.kill('SIGKILL');
  await new Promise(resolve => { if (child.exitCode !== null || child.signalCode !== null) resolve(); else child.once('exit', () => resolve()); });
  rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

## Actual

Bun 1.4.3: `{"destroyed":true,"exit":"alive","ticksAfterClose":29}`.

Node v26.7.0: `{"destroyed":true,"exit":0,"ticksAfterClose":1}`.

Bun marks both parent read streams destroyed, yet the child continues successfully writing to both descriptors. The child explicitly handles SIGPIPE and EPIPE, so the comparison does not depend on the runtimes' default SIGPIPE policies. An independent Python OS-level parent closing the actual pipe readers causes the same Bun child to exit promptly.

