### What steps can reproduce the bug?

While a `bun:test` matcher waits on a pending promise (`await expect(promise).resolves` / `.rejects`), `setImmediate` callbacks scheduled by that promise's work are only run every few hundred milliseconds, **as long as the process has any other open I/O handle** (an idle child process with piped stdio, or a listening TCP server, is enough). The same code awaited directly runs each hop in well under a millisecond.

`repro.test.js`:

```js
import { test, expect } from "bun:test";
import { spawn } from "node:child_process";
import { createServer } from "node:net";

const hops = async (n) => {
  const t = performance.now();
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) await new Promise((resolve) => setImmediate(resolve));
  return performance.now() - t;
};

const measure = async () => {
  const plain = await hops(10);
  let resolvesMs = 0;
  await expect((async () => { resolvesMs = await hops(10); })()).resolves.toBeUndefined();
  let rejectsMs = 0;
  const err = new Error("expected");
  await expect((async () => { rejectsMs = await hops(10); throw err; })()).rejects.toBe(err);
  return `plain await ${plain.toFixed(1)}ms | inside expect().resolves ${resolvesMs.toFixed(1)}ms | inside expect().rejects ${rejectsMs.toFixed(1)}ms`;
};

test("no extra handles", async () => {
  console.log(`Bun ${Bun.version} [no handles]    10 setImmediate hops: ${await measure()}`);
});

test("with a live child process (piped stdio)", async () => {
  const child = spawn("sleep", ["30"], { stdio: ["pipe", "pipe", "pipe"] });
  try {
    console.log(`Bun ${Bun.version} [child process] 10 setImmediate hops: ${await measure()}`);
  } finally {
    child.kill();
  }
});

test("with a listening TCP server", async () => {
  const server = createServer();
  await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
  try {
    console.log(`Bun ${Bun.version} [tcp server]    10 setImmediate hops: ${await measure()}`);
  } finally {
    server.close();
  }
}, 30000);
```

Run: `bun test ./repro.test.js --timeout 30000`

### What is the expected behavior?

`setImmediate` hops take the same time whether the promise is awaited directly or through `expect(...).resolves` / `.rejects`, i.e. well under 1 ms for 10 hops, regardless of other open handles (this is what the "no handles" case shows).

