### Additional information

`mmap_file` opens the file read-write regardless of options. In [`src/sys/lib.rs` at the `bun-v1.4.4` tag, line 3446](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/blob/bun-v1.4.4/src/sys/lib.rs#L3446):

```rust
let fd = open(path, O::RDWR, 0)?;
```

**Why this matters.** A common deployment is a large prebuilt data file shipped in a container image and mapped at startup so the kernel can share and evict its pages. The files are typically root-owned and read-only, or on a read-only rootfs.

Requiring write access breaks that. On a writable overlay filesystem, opening a file in a lower image layer read-write also makes the overlay copy the whole file into the container layer before mapping. Memory is then duplicated per container instead of shared from the image.

**Suggestion.** Open with `O_RDONLY` and map with `PROT_READ` when the mapping cannot write back to the file, or add a `readonly` / `writable: false` option. `MAP_PRIVATE` with `PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE` is also allowed on an `O_RDONLY` fd, since copy-on-write pages never reach the file. So only `shared: true` with write access needs `O_RDWR`.

