### What happens

With `node:http`, if a client request is destroyed with an error (`req.destroy(err)`) while the response body is still arriving, Bun emits `'end'` on the response with only the bytes received so far. `res.complete` is `false`, but no `'error'` is emitted and the request does not reject.

Node.js emits an error in the same situation, so code that collects the body on `'end'` gets a truncated body under Bun and treats it as complete. In our case this surfaced as `JSON Parse error: Unterminated string` from a library that parses the body on `'end'`.

### Reproduction

```ts
import http from "node:http";

// Server: headers plus half a JSON body, then the rest after 500 ms.
const srv = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "application/json" });
  res.write('{"Documents":[{"name":"abcdefghij');
  setTimeout(() => res.end('klmnop"}]}'), 500);
}).listen(0, async () => {
  const port = (srv.address() as any).port;
  const ac = new AbortController();
  const p = new Promise<string>((resolve, reject) => {
    const req = http.request({ port, path: "/", method: "GET" }, (res) => {
      const buf: Buffer[] = [];
      res.on("data", (c) => buf.push(Buffer.from(c)));
      res.on("end", () => resolve(Buffer.concat(buf).toString()));
      res.on("error", reject);
      res.on("close", () => console.log("close, complete =", res.complete));
    });
    req.once("error", reject);
    ac.signal.addEventListener("abort", () => req.destroy(new Error("aborted")));
    req.end();
  });
  setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 150);
  try {
    const body = await p;
    console.log("resolved with:", body);
    JSON.parse(body);
  } catch (e: any) {
    console.log("error:", e.message);
  }
  srv.close();
  process.exit(0);
});
```

**Bun 1.3.11 (macOS 26.6, arm64):**
```
