### Additional information

It only happens when the private member itself is decorated (`@dec #p` or `@dec accessor #p`). An undecorated `#p` in a class that has other decorated members works. A plain `this.#p = v` also works, because the lowering turns it into `__privateSet`. Every other write position breaks:

| In a class with `@dec accessor #p = 1` | Bun result |
|---|---|
| `this.#p = 5` | ✅ |
| `this.#p += 1` | `SyntaxError: Left side of assignment is not a reference.` |
| `this.#p++` | `SyntaxError: Postfix ++ operator applied to value that is not a reference.` |
| `({ 0: this.#p } = arr)` / `({ x: this.#p } = obj)` | `SyntaxError: Invalid destructuring assignment target.` |
| `[this.#p] = arr` / `[...this.#p] = arr` / `({ ...this.#p } = obj)` | `SyntaxError: Invalid destructuring assignment target.` |
| `for (this.#p of arr)` / `for (this.#p in obj)` | `SyntaxError: Cannot reference undeclared private names: "#p"` |

Public decorated accessors (`@dec accessor x`, then `({0: this.x} = arr)`) work, and so does the same code in `.ts` files.

