The generated parent directories do not exist; the final basename is always `s.sock`. Each line prints the path length in bytes and the error code.

### What is the expected behavior?

Bun's long-path workaround should preserve `ENOENT` from the failed parent-directory lookup. The Node compatibility mismatch is visible at 104 and 108 bytes on macOS, and exactly 108 bytes on Linux.

### What do you see instead?

Boundary comparison from the same missing-parent scenario at several path lengths:

| Path bytes | Bun macOS | Node.js macOS | Bun Linux | Node.js Linux |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 100 | ENOENT | ENOENT | ENOENT | ENOENT |
| 104 | EINVAL | ENOENT | ENOENT | ENOENT |
| 108 | EINVAL | ENOENT | EINVAL | ENOENT |
| 140 | EINVAL | ENOENT | EINVAL | EINVAL |

Node also rejects the 140-byte Linux path; that row is not a Node compatibility mismatch.

In Bun, creating the parent restores `ENOENT` for an absent socket, and live sockets at these lengths connect successfully. The incorrect `EINVAL` breaks clients that start a daemon on `ENOENT`.

### Additional information

**Thresholds:** The OS headers define `sun_path` as [104 bytes on macOS](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/bsd/sys/un.h) and [108 bytes on Linux](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/include/uapi/linux/un.h). Bun enters its long-path workaround when the path reaches `sizeof(sun_path)`.

