### What steps can reproduce the bug?

`repro.mjs`:

```js
import { writeSync } from "node:fs";

// The Node idiom CLIs use before handing fd 1 to synchronous or native writers.
if (!process.stdout.isTTY) process.stdout._handle?.setBlocking?.(true);
process.stderr.write(`process.stdout._handle: ${typeof process.stdout._handle}\n`);

const data = Buffer.alloc(1_048_576, "x");
let written = 0;
try {
  while (written < data.length) written += writeSync(1, data, written);
} catch (error) {
  process.stderr.write(`${error.code} after ${written} bytes\n`);
  process.exitCode = 1;
}
```

```sh
bun repro.mjs | (sleep 0.3; wc -c)
node repro.mjs | (sleep 0.3; wc -c)
```

### What is the expected behavior?

Node v24.22.1 writes all 1,048,576 bytes. `process.stdout._handle.setBlocking(true)` clears `O_NONBLOCK` on the stdout pipe, so later synchronous writes to fd 1 block until the reader drains them.

```
process.stdout._handle: object
1048576
```

### What do you see instead?

```
process.stdout._handle: undefined
EAGAIN after 8192 bytes
8192
```

Touching `process.stdout` sets `O_NONBLOCK` on fd 1 in both runtimes. We checked this with `/proc/self/fdinfo/1`. Bun has no `process.stdout._handle`, so the `setBlocking(true)` call is a silent no-op and fd 1 stays non-blocking. Any later synchronous or native write to fd 1 gets `EAGAIN` once the pipe is full.

### Additional information

A real case is Oxfmt 0.61.0 run with `bun --bun ./node_modules/oxfmt/bin/oxfmt --stdin-filepath x.json < big.json | wc -c`. Its `dist/cli.js` uses this idiom, and its native binding writes the formatted output to fd 1. `strace` shows `write(1, …, 1254805) = 8192` followed by `write(1, …) = -1 EAGAIN`. Oxfmt then exits 0 with 8,192 of 1,254,805 bytes. Under Node it writes the full output.

Related but different: #20562 and #25432 (64 KB truncation through `Bun.stdout.write` and a saved `process.stdout.write`, both closed) and #40298 (Oxfmt under `--bun`).

